//   means a possible collision course; landing lights within 10 miles and below 10,000 feet; tap the position light fixtures; hold the brakes
//   in the run-up; climb on instruments; no turns until a safe altitude; disappearing ground lights and halos; a low, shallow approach is
//   inappropriate at night; round out when tire marks show in the landing light; without landing lights, round out when the far end lights
//   rise above the nose; blackout landings; the night engine failure list; "even for experienced pilots, night VFR operations should only be
//   conducted in unrestricted visibility").
//   Chapter 18: https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/regulations_policies/handbooks_manuals/aviation/airplane_handbook/19_afh_ch18.pdf
//   (forced versus precautionary landing; wishful thinking; dispensable structure; doubling groundspeed quadruples the energy; 9G and the 9.4
//   and 37.6 foot figures; terrain selection, change the plan no more than once; flaps timing; master off only when power is no longer needed;
//   power lines along roads; straight ahead after an engine failure on takeoff; emergency descent with 30 to 45 degrees of bank; engine fire:
//   mixture idle cutoff and fuel selector off first, ignition left on, master switch caution; electrical fire; cabin fire and vents only after
//   the extinguisher; alternator failure: the 25 amp hour battery example, shed load, land at the nearest suitable airport; door opening;
//   inadvertent VFR into IMC: keep the wings level, trust the instruments, untrained pilots lose control in about 10 minutes).
// Private Pilot Airplane ACS, FAA-S-ACS-6C, PA.XI.A (K1 to K8, R1 to R7), PA.IX.A to IX.D (including K2a to K2c best glide, minimum sink,
//   wind; K5 ELT; IX.D.R1 survival gear for 48 to 72 hours), PA.I.H: https://www.faa.gov/training_testing/testing/acs/private_airplane_acs_6.pdf
// Sun and moon: U.S. Naval Observatory one-day API, coordinates 39.5701 N, 104.8493 W (KAPA), UTC minus 7:
//   https://aa.usno.navy.mil/api/rstt/oneday?date=2026-11-25&coords=39.5701,-104.8493&tz=-7 (and 2026-12-03, 2026-12-13; KPUB for 2026-12-13).
//   Nov 25 (Wed): sunset 4:38 PM, end of civil twilight 5:07 PM, moon 98 percent rising 5:29 PM. Dec 3 (Thu): sunset 4:35, end of civil
//   twilight 5:05, moon 25 percent and waning, risen at 1:51 AM that morning and not up again until 2:55 AM on Dec 4 (a dark evening). Dec 13 (Sun): sunset 4:36, end of civil twilight 5:06; KPUB sunset 4:38,
//   end 5:08.
// Airports, AirNav (FAA information effective 01 October 2026): https://www.airnav.com/airport/KPUB , https://www.airnav.com/airport/KAPA ,
//   https://www.airnav.com/airport/KCFO . KPUB Pueblo Memorial, 38-17-23.8N 104-29-52.9W, 4,729 ft, tower 0600 to 2200, white-green beacon,
//   4-light PAPIs, pilot-activated lights when the tower is closed. KAPA lights sunset to sunrise, 35R 4-light PAPI on the left and MALSR.
//   KCFO tower 0700 to 2100, runway 8/26 high intensity edge lights activated on the common frequency, 2-light PAPIs on 8 and 26.
// Distances computed from the AirNav coordinates (haversine): KAPA to KPUB 78.6 NM direct; KAPA to Monument 28.8 and Monument to KPUB 51.2,
//   so 80 NM by I-25 and 160 NM round trip. Wind solutions for the fuel drill: TAS 105, wind 340 true at 13: southbound GS 118, northbound 92.
// Invented for the story: every METAR, TAF, PIREP, FB line, and controller call; the upslope storm; Biscuit's glide card (a planning card in the
//   style of a 172N POH, 65 KIAS and about 9 to 1, not quoted from a POH); the club planning numbers carried from chapter 9 (105 KTAS, 8.0
//   gal/hr, 1.0 gallon taxi and runup and 1.0 gallon climb per departure, 40 gallons usable); Lourdes's birthday, December 13 (her 63rd), her
//   home in Aurora, her Pueblo childhood, and Velarde's, a restaurant in Pueblo that doesn't exist; Walt's dispatch story (a regional outfit,
//   a thirty-some seat turboprop flown under Part 121, and a mountain town, all unnamed and fictional; no real airline, accident, or person); every person's actions. Jordan's shift
//   starts in this stretch: Nov 23, 26, 29, Dec 2, 5, 8, 11, 14 (7 AM to 7 AM). MST = UTC minus 7. No frequencies appear on purpose; the guard frequency is
//   "the emergency frequency."
(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).pilot=window.HALCYON_DATA.pilot||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.pilot.full||[]).push({
  num: 10, part: `Part Two: The Trip`, title: `The No-Go`, weeks: `November to December`,
  tasks: [`XI`,`IX`,`I.H`,`I.B`,`III.A`,`I.D`,`I.C`,`VIII`],
  opening: [
    `Monday, November 23, eleven at night, the station kitchen, a quiet hour on a twenty four. Jordan had Abby's text open on her phone and the regulations open on the station laptop, which was against some policy nobody had ever explained to her. Three hours of night. Ten full stops. One cross-country of over a hundred nautical miles, total distance.`,
    `Her mother's text had come in at 6:15 and she'd been carrying it around all shift like a stone in a shoe.`,
    `<prop type="text" from="Lourdes Reyes" time="6:15 PM">Sunday the 13th. Velarde's at 6:30. Same as always. Claire is driving me and the baby. You come too.</prop>`,
    `Velarde's was in Pueblo. It had been in Pueblo since before her mother was born there, a long room with red vinyl booths and a back room that the family took over on the Saturday nearest December 13 most years, and on December 13 itself when it fell on a weekend, whether or not the restaurant had planned on it. Her mother would be sixty three. It would be the first birthday without Danny.`,
    `And Danny had flown to it. Every year since he got his certificate, he'd flown Biscuit or a rental down to Pueblo Memorial, and somebody's cousin had picked him up in a truck, and Lourdes had sat in the back room with her phone face up on the table next to her plate until it buzzed. Jordan had driven. Every year. She'd been the one who brought the cake in the trunk.`,
    `She pulled up the chart on the laptop and measured it with the cursor because she didn't have the plotter with her. Centennial to Pueblo by I-25, about 80 miles. There and back, 160. Over a hundred. She looked at it for a while, the way you look at a number on a monitor you've already decided you don't like.`,
    `<prop type="text" from="Jordan Reyes" time="11:09 PM">What if the night cross-country is Pueblo. Sunday Dec 13. Down at 5:15, back at 9.</prop>`,
    `Abby answered at 11:10, which meant Abby was also awake for reasons.`,
    `<prop type="text" from="Abby Strand" time="11:10 PM">Pueblo's 80. Round trip 160. It works on paper. What's in Pueblo.</prop>`,
    `<prop type="text" from="Jordan Reyes" time="11:12 PM">My mom's birthday dinner. Danny always flew down.</prop>`,
    `Three dots for a long time. Then: <b>Ok. Then you have a deadline and a reason, which is two more things than the airplane needs. We'll talk about that. Night one is Wednesday. Be at the hangar at 4:15. Before sunset. I mean it.</b>`,
    `Claire texted at 11:30, because apparently nobody in her life slept. <b>El wants to know if she can ride down in Biscuit with you. I said ask Aunt Jordan. I'm sorry.</b>`,
    `She wrote back the rule, because it was easier than the rest of it. <b>Training flights don't carry passengers. And when I'm alone, student pilots can't. Not even El.</b> Claire sent back a picture of Ellie's face reading the text, which said everything about the regulation that needed saying.`,
    `Later, at two, with the rig restocked and nobody calling, she took the green notebook out of her flight bag and paged through the back half. She wasn't looking for anything. Then she was. December, five years ago. The first line said KPUB, and Ma, and a number that would have been her mother's age. She didn't read the rest. She folded the corner down, the way she'd done with his first cross-country, and put the notebook back in the bag. His, after hers. That was the rule.`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Abby's Ground School: after dark, and when it goes wrong`,
    sections: [
      { h: `Three kinds of night`,
        body: [`The regulations can't agree on when it's dark, so you're going to learn three answers and when each one applies. The test loves this, and so do examiners.`,
          `The first one is the definition. 14 CFR 1.1 says night is the time between the end of evening civil twilight and the beginning of morning civil twilight, as published in the Air Almanac, converted to local time. Civil twilight ends when the center of the sun is 6 degrees below the horizon. Out here in late November that's about half an hour after sunset. On Wednesday the sun sets at 4:38 and civil twilight ends at 5:07. That's the night that counts for logging night time and for the night training your certificate requires.`,
          `The second one is for lights. 91.209 says that from sunset to sunrise you can't operate an aircraft unless its position lights are on. Sunset, not twilight. So at 4:39 on Wednesday the lights have to be on even though it isn't legally night yet.`,
          `The third one is for passengers. 61.57(b) says you can't act as pilot in command of an aircraft carrying persons during the period beginning 1 hour after sunset and ending 1 hour before sunrise unless, within the preceding 90 days, you've made at least three takeoffs and three landings to a full stop during that same period, as the sole manipulator of the controls, in the same category and class. On Wednesday that window opens at 5:38. A landing at 5:20 counts as a night landing in your logbook and doesn't count for 61.57. A touch and go doesn't count for 61.57 at all. Full stop.`,
          `You won't need 61.57 until you have a certificate, because a student can't carry anybody. But you'll need it the first winter you want to bring Ellie home from somewhere after dinner, so learn it now, while it's free.`],
        terms: [[`Night (1.1)`,`End of evening civil twilight to beginning of morning civil twilight. Used for logging night time and night training.`],[`Position lights (91.209)`,`Required from sunset to sunrise.`],[`Night currency (61.57(b))`,`To carry passengers from 1 hour after sunset to 1 hour before sunrise: 3 takeoffs and 3 full-stop landings in that period within 90 days.`]],
        exam: `Expect three different questions with three different answers: the definition of night, when lights are required, and the period for passenger currency. 61.57(b) landings must be to a full stop.` },
      { h: `What you need, and what you can't do`,
        body: [`61.109(a)(2) says your private certificate needs 3 hours of night flight training in a single-engine airplane, and that has to include one cross-country flight of over 100 nautical miles total distance and 10 takeoffs and 10 landings to a full stop, each one with a trip around the traffic pattern, at an airport. Training means me in the right seat. None of the night requirement for the private is solo.`,
          `Read that cross-country line carefully, because it's different from the one you flew alone. Over 100 nautical miles total distance. Pueblo and back by I-25 is about 160. For the hours to count as cross-country time under 61.1, the landing still has to be at a point more than 50 nautical miles straight line from where you started. Pueblo is 78.6.`,
          `Now the limits. On a dual flight I'm pilot in command, so 61.89 isn't what keeps Ellie home. I am. Training flights carry the trainee and the trainer, and that's it. 61.89 is for when you're PIC: as a student, no passengers, ever. Your mother wasn't going to ride along anyway, which I gather was never a risk. 61.89 also says a student can't be pilot in command with flight or surface visibility under 3 statute miles during the day or 5 at night, or when the flight can't be made with visual reference to the surface. Five at night. Remember the number. The night takes things away from you, and the rule asks for more room because of it.`,
          `Could you fly at night alone? Only after 61.87(o): night training at the airport you'll solo from, with takeoffs, approaches, landings, and go-arounds, and night navigation training near it, plus an endorsement for this make and model from the instructor who gave that training within the 90 days before the flight. The advisory circular has the wording, and it expires 90 days after the training. I'm not planning to give you that one. Your night is going to be with me.`],
        terms: [[`61.109(a)(2)`,`3 hours night training: one cross-country over 100 NM total distance, and 10 takeoffs and 10 full-stop landings in the pattern.`],[`61.89(a)(1)`,`A student pilot may not act as PIC of an aircraft carrying a passenger.`],[`61.89(a)(6)`,`No student PIC with visibility under 3 SM by day or 5 SM at night.`],[`61.87(o)`,`Student solo at night needs night training at that airport, night navigation training nearby, and a make and model endorsement within 90 days.`]],
        exam: `The private needs 3 hours of night training with an instructor, including a cross-country over 100 NM total and 10 full-stop landings. A student acting as PIC carries no passengers, day or night.` },
      { h: `Lights on the airplane`,
        body: [`91.205(c) is the night equipment list, and it sits on top of the day list you already know. Everything required for day VFR, plus approved position lights, an approved red or white anticollision light system, an electric landing light if the airplane is operated for hire, an adequate source of electrical energy for all the installed electrical and radio equipment, and one spare set of fuses, or three spare fuses of each kind, where the pilot can reach them in flight. People remember it as FLAPS: fuses, landing light if for hire, anticollision lights, position lights, a source of power. Biscuit has breakers, not fuses, so skip that letter. And the landing light's only required for hire. Nobody's paying me enough to count. We use it anyway.`,
          `Strobe dies in flight? 91.205 says you can keep going to somewhere it can get fixed. 91.209 lets you, the pilot in command, turn the strobes off when they're making things worse, like in haze, where they flash back in your face. Position lights don't get that deal. They're required, and 91.213 won't let you placard your way past anything 91.205 requires for the flight you're about to fly. Red light out on the left wing? Congratulations, you own a day airplane.`,
          `Same layout as a boat. Red on the left tip, green on the right tip, white on the tail. Here's the test question, straight out of the handbook. Another airplane's red is on your left and its green is on your right: it's headed the way you are. Keep your spacing. Red on your right, green on your left: that's its face. You might be about to meet. One white light by itself is somebody's tail, leaving.`,
          `The AFH wants landing lights on within 10 miles of an airport and below 10,000 feet, day or night. It also says to knock on every position light during the preflight. Blinks when you knock? Find out why before you leave.`],
        terms: [[`91.205(c) (FLAPS)`,`Day VFR equipment plus fuses (if used), landing light (if for hire), anticollision lights, position lights, adequate electrical source.`],[`Position lights`,`Red left wingtip, green right wingtip, white tail.`],[`Collision cue`,`Red on the right and green on the left: you're looking at its nose.`],[`91.213(d)`,`Can't defer equipment required by 91.205 for that kind of flight.`]],
        exam: `Know the 91.205(c) additions for night, and the position light geometry. A question that shows red on the right, green on the left is asking you to recognize a head-on.` },
      { h: `Lights on the ground`,
        body: [`Beacons first. White, green, white, green: lighted civilian land airport. White and yellow: seaplane base, which you won't be needing. Military flashes two quick whites, then green. Ours turns from sunset to sunrise. Now and then you'll see one turning in broad daylight in a Class B, C, D or E surface area, and the AIM says that often means visibility under 3 or ceiling under 1,000, and you'd need a clearance. Then it says don't rely on the beacon alone. Classic AIM.`,
          `Runway edges are white. Instrument runways go yellow for the last 2,000 feet, or the last half if the runway's short, so you know you're running out. The end lights are two-faced. Green faces out, toward you on final, and that's the threshold. Red faces in, toward whoever's rolling out, and that's the end. Taxiway edges, blue. Taxiway centerlines, if they've got them, green. Outside the blue is ground nobody promised you anything about.`,
          `PAPI. Two or four lights in a row next to the runway, usually the left side. Four-light version: two white, two red, you're on the glide path. Three white, bit high. All white, high. Three red, bit low. All red, low, and low is the one with poles in it. The AIM gives you obstacle clearance within 10 degrees either side of centerline out to 3.4 miles, and it says don't start down on it until you're lined up. The older VASI is two bars. Red over white, you're all right. White over white, too high. Red over red, you're dead. Pilots made that one up, not me.`,
          `Some airports keep the lights off until you ask. That's pilot controlled lighting, on whatever frequency the Chart Supplement lists. Click the mic 7 times inside 5 seconds for high, 5 for medium, 3 for low. Seven first. Always seven first, says the AIM, because on some systems three won't wake up everything, and you can dial it down after. There's a 15 minute timer, and it restarts on your last click. Hit it again overhead or turning final. Otherwise the runway can shut off with you a hundred feet over the numbers. Yes, me. No, you can't have the story.`],
        terms: [[`Beacon`,`White and green: lighted civilian land airport. Daytime operation in a Class B, C, D or E surface area often means below 3 SM or 1,000 ft.`],[`Edge lights`,`White; yellow on the last 2,000 ft of instrument runways. Threshold green outward, end red inward. Taxiways blue.`],[`PAPI`,`2 white 2 red on path. More red is low. Obstruction clearance within 10 degrees either side of centerline, out to 3.4 NM.`],[`Pilot controlled lighting`,`7, 5, or 3 clicks within 5 seconds. 15 minutes. Key 7 first.`]],
        exam: `PAPI and VASI color questions are nearly free points. So is the 7, 5, 3 click sequence and the 15 minutes.` },
      { h: `Your eyes at night`,
        body: [`You know this better than I do, probably, but here's the pilot version. Cones: color, detail, packed into the middle of the retina, the fovea. Rods: dim light, no color, out in the edges. The handbook puts adapted rods at something like 10,000 times as sensitive as cones, which sounds great until you hear the catch. The fovea has no rods. So at night you've got a blind spot 5 to 10 degrees wide parked exactly where you point your eyes. Look right at a dim light, a tail light way out, and sometimes it's just gone.`,
          `Fix: look beside it. About 10 degrees off, above or below or to the side, whichever. And keep moving, because the PHAK says even that picture fades in 2 or 3 seconds if you hold still on it. For traffic, chop the sky into pieces, 10 degrees per piece at most, a second or more on each one. That's the AIM's version and it's a good one.`,
          `Getting your night eyes takes about half an hour for the whole set, per the AIM and both handbooks, or about 20 minutes to a decent amount under dim red light. Losing them takes one second of something bright. Ramp floodlight, somebody's landing light swinging across you on the taxiway: shut one eye. That one stays dark adapted and you'll be glad. Strobes are too quick to hurt it. Phones are not. You're going to dim your phone all the way and then dim it again.`,
          `Red light keeps your adaptation, and it ruins your chart. Under red light, magenta washes out into the paper and blue things go dark, and half of what's printed on a sectional is magenta. The AFH's answer is a flashlight with both a red and a white light: white for the walkaround, red in the cockpit, dim white when you need to read the chart. Bring two, and spare batteries.`,
          `Things that make night vision worse: altitude, because the AIM says it's impaired above about 5,000 feet of cabin altitude and Centennial is 5,885, which is why the books recommend oxygen above 5,000 at night. Carbon monoxide, including from smoking. Low vitamin A. Prolonged bright sun earlier in the day. My rule, not the book's: if you're flying tonight, wear sunglasses all day. And everything on IMSAFE, especially fatigue.`],
        terms: [[`Night blind spot`,`No rods in the fovea. Look 5 to 10 degrees off center.`],[`Dark adaptation`,`About 30 minutes for full; moderate in 20 under dim red light. Lost quickly to bright light.`],[`Off-center viewing`,`Look about 10 degrees beside an object; don't hold one spot more than 2 to 3 seconds.`],[`Red light`,`Preserves adaptation but distorts chart colors, especially magenta.`]],
        exam: `Expect off-center viewing, 30 minutes of dark adaptation, and the 5,000 foot night oxygen recommendation. "Look directly at it" is never the right answer at night.` },
      { h: `Illusions after dark`,
        body: [`You met most of the illusions in the human factors chapter. Night brings out the ones that only need your eyes to fool you.`,
          `False horizon. Ground lights and stars smeared together, or a tilted line of lights, and your brain grabs the wrong line and calls it level. Out east, a road going up a hill will do it to you every time. You'll drop a wing to match and feel great about it. Autokinesis. Stare at one light on a dark background, and after 8 or 10 seconds it starts wandering around. People have followed it into the dirt. Same cure for both. Keep your eyes moving, and when outside stops making sense, fly the attitude indicator.`,
          `Landing ones. Featureless ground under final, like water, a dark field, snow, makes you feel high, so you come in low. That's the black hole approach: runway lights, and under you, nothing. Bright runway lights with dark all around make the runway seem closer, so you come in high. A straight row of lights on a road, or a train, can pass for a runway. Rain on the windshield, you feel high. Haze, the runway looks far off. Rule of thumb: whatever makes you feel high makes you fly low. And low is where the poles live.`,
          `Flicker vertigo is the weird one. Light flickering 4 to 20 times a second, a strobe chopped up by the prop, say, can make some people dizzy or sick or worse. Something flickering at you? Make it stop.`,
          `Fixing it is boring. Use the PAPI. Look at the altimeter on every approach. Learn the airport before you show up, from the Chart Supplement, and if you can, from flying it in daylight first. And the AFH says a low, shallow approach is definitely inappropriate at night. Definitely. I'd take the hint.`],
        terms: [[`False horizon`,`Ground lights, stars, or sloping clouds mistaken for the horizon.`],[`Autokinesis`,`A stationary light seems to move after 8 to 10 seconds of staring.`],[`Black hole approach`,`Over dark terrain toward a lighted runway. Feels high, so the pilot flies low.`],[`Bright runway lights`,`Runway seems closer. Pilot tends to fly high.`],[`Flicker vertigo`,`Light flickering at 4 to 20 cycles per second.`]],
        exam: `Featureless terrain and darkness produce a low approach. Bright lights produce a high one. Autokinesis comes from staring at a single light.` },
      { h: `Flying it`,
        body: [`The airplane doesn't know it's dark. Everything about how it flies is the same. What changes is you, and what you can see, so the procedures lean harder on the instruments.`,
          `Preflight by daylight when you can. Check every light, tap the position lights, and walk the ramp with the flashlight for chocks, ladders, and tie down ropes. Organize the cockpit before you start: flashlight where your hand can find it, chart folded, checklist out. Clear the propeller area with extra care and turn the beacon on so anybody nearby knows. Taxi slowly. In the run-up, hold the brakes and watch for creeping, because at night you won't notice the airplane rolling forward until it's on the hold line.`,
          `On takeoff, the runway lights keep you straight, and then they're behind you and there's nothing ahead. Fly the attitude indicator, the altimeter, and the vertical speed indicator until they all agree you're climbing. The AFH says inexperienced pilots sometimes relax right rudder after liftoff and drift left, and that you shouldn't turn until you're at a safe altitude. Turn the landing light off once the climb is established if it's reflecting off haze.`,
          `In cruise, you can't see clouds at night. The AFH says the first sign is usually the ground lights fading out. If they start to get halos, that's moisture, and going on in the same direction calls for caution. The handbook's summary of the whole chapter is the sentence I want you to remember: even for experienced pilots, night VFR should only be flown in unrestricted visibility, favorable winds on the surface and aloft, and no turbulence. Even for experienced pilots.`,
          `On approach, find the beacon and fly toward it until you can pick out the runway lights from everything else. Fly a normal pattern at normal speeds, cross-check the altimeter on every leg, and use the PAPI. Landing light on about halfway down final. Start the round out when the light shows you the tire marks on the runway. If the landing light fails, start the round out when the lights at the far end of the runway appear to rise above the nose, and feel for the runway with a little power. We'll practice that one. The AFH says blackout landings belong in every night pilot's training.`],
        terms: [[`Night preflight`,`Check and tap the lights, walk the ramp with a flashlight, organize the cockpit.`],[`Night takeoff`,`Climb on instruments: attitude, altimeter, VSI. No turns until a safe altitude.`],[`Clouds at night`,`First sign is ground lights fading or haloing.`],[`No landing light`,`Round out when the far end lights rise above the nose.`]],
        exam: `Night takeoffs lean on the flight instruments. The first sign of cloud at night is ground lights disappearing.` },
      { h: `When the engine quits`,
        body: [`Fly the airplane first. Always, day or night. Pitch for best glide, trim for it, and then think. Biscuit's best glide is 65 knots indicated, flaps up. The club's glide card says that from 1,000 feet above the ground, in still air, she'll go about a mile and a half, which is roughly 9 to 1. A headwind takes some of that away and a tailwind gives some back, in proportion to what it does to your groundspeed. Best glide is the speed for the most distance. Minimum sink is a little slower and keeps you in the air the longest, but covers less ground. If you need to get somewhere, fly best glide.`,
          `Then the handbook's terms. A forced landing is immediate, because you can't keep flying, like an engine failure. A precautionary landing is one you choose when you still could keep flying but shouldn't, for weather, fuel, being lost, or an engine running rough. The AFH warns that too many precautionary landings turn into forced ones because the pilot used wishful thinking instead of reason. The noninstrument pilot trapped by weather is the example it uses.`,
          `Picking a place. Height decides how much choice you have. From high up, pick a general area first and then a spot. The handbook says you can discard a plan for one that's obviously better, and that you shouldn't change your mind more than once. A well-flown landing in poor terrain beats an uncontrolled one in a perfect field. Land into the wind if you can, at the slowest speed you can control, because doubling your groundspeed quadruples the energy that has to go somewhere. Let the airplane's wings and gear absorb it. The airplane is the dispensable part. Roads look tempting, and most of them have power lines along them that you won't see until late.`,
          `At night the AFH adds a list. Keep control and set up best glide. Turn toward an airport, or away from built up areas. Look for the cause: fuel selector, mixture, magnetos, primer. Tell ATC, and if you're already talking to someone, don't change frequencies unless they say to. If you know the terrain, plan for an unlighted part of it, close to a road or public access if you can, so help can get to you. Know the wind. Run the before landing checklist, test the landing light at altitude, and turn it on in time to see what's ahead. If you have no light and no outside reference, hold a level landing attitude until you touch. Then switches off and get out.`,
          `Partial power is more common than total. Roughness in the O-320 on a cool, damp night is carb ice until proven otherwise, so carb heat on and leave it on. Then the fuel selector, the mixture, the mags one at a time, and the primer locked. A sick engine that's still making some power is a gift. Use it to get to a better field, and plan the landing like it might quit on final.`],
        terms: [[`Best glide`,`Biscuit: 65 KIAS flaps up. Club card: about 1.5 NM per 1,000 ft AGL in still air (about 9 to 1).`],[`Minimum sink`,`Slower than best glide. Longest time aloft, less distance.`],[`Forced landing`,`Immediate landing because flight can't continue.`],[`Precautionary landing`,`A chosen landing when continued flight is possible but inadvisable.`],[`Night engine failure`,`Best glide, toward an airport or away from congested areas, find the cause, tell ATC, unlighted area near access, landing light on in time.`]],
        exam: `Expect best glide versus minimum sink, the effect of wind on glide distance, and forced versus precautionary landing.` },
      { h: `Fire, smoke, and electricity`,
        body: [`Engine fire in flight. The AFH says unless the manufacturer says otherwise, the first step is to shut off the fuel: mixture to idle cutoff, fuel selector off. Leave the ignition on so the engine burns what's left in the lines. If the fire goes out, don't restart. Then you're gliding, and you know what to do. Some checklists say master off too. Biscuit's has it third, which is why your mayday goes out before you get there. The handbook points out that unless it's an electrical fire or you're about to touch down, that also kills your radios and your transponder. A single-engine airplane with an engine fire is landing, and soon, and the handbook's line is the right one: the airplane is expendable, and the only thing that matters is the people in it. An emergency descent, if you need one, is power idle and a bank of about 30 to 45 degrees to keep the wings loaded, within the airplane's speed limits.`,
          `Electrical fire smells like hot insulation. Try to find the circuit by checking breakers and switches. If you can't, and the flying allows it, master and alternator off. If you really need power, the handbook gives a way to find the bad circuit, and then says the most prudent thing is to land as soon as possible.`,
          `Cabin fire: use the extinguisher first, and only then open the vents to clear the smoke. Opening the vents first feeds it, and if the smoke gets worse when you open them, close them. Biscuit's extinguisher clips under the left seat. Know how to pull the pin without looking.`,
          `Alternator failure, which Marisol asked you about in May. The ammeter shows a steady discharge, the needle sitting left of center by about what the airplane is drawing, a few amps up to maybe twenty, and now the battery's carrying everything. The AFH's example is a 25 amp hour battery that might give 5 amps for 5 hours, or only about 10 to 15 minutes at 40 amps, and less if it's old. So shed everything you don't need, right away. Tell ATC. Land at the nearest suitable airport. At night, nearest suitable means lit and open, and if the lights are on a radio switch, it means one where you'll still have a radio when you get there.`],
        terms: [[`Engine fire`,`Mixture idle cutoff, fuel selector off, ignition on. Don't restart. Forced landing.`],[`Electrical fire`,`Hot insulation smell. Isolate the circuit, or master off if flight allows. Land soon.`],[`Cabin fire`,`Extinguisher first, then vents. Close them if smoke worsens.`],[`Alternator failure`,`Shed load, tell ATC, land at the nearest suitable airport.`]],
        exam: `The first action for an in-flight engine fire is to shut off fuel to the engine. For an alternator failure, it's reducing the electrical load.` },
      { h: `Asking for help`,
        body: [`The AIM has a sentence I'd like on your wall. An aircraft is in at least an urgency condition the moment the pilot becomes doubtful about position, fuel endurance, weather, or any other condition that could hurt the flight. Doubtful. Not sure you're in trouble. Doubtful. That's the time to ask, before it's a distress. The AIM says delay has caused accidents and cost lives.`,
          `Distress is MAYDAY, urgency is PAN-PAN, each said three times if you can. Then who you're calling, who you are, what's wrong, the weather, what you want, where you are, your altitude, fuel in minutes, and people aboard. If you're already talking to a controller and have a code, keep it unless they tell you otherwise, because they already know where you are. If you can't reach anyone, squawk 7700, and keep calling. You know 7600 and 7500 from the last chapter. 91.3 says the pilot in command may deviate from the rules as much as an emergency requires, and send a written report if the FAA asks. Nobody's going to violate you for saving your own life.`,
          `There's one frequency that military towers, most civil towers and radar facilities listen to, called the emergency frequency, and you'll find it in the Chart Supplement. Direction finding stations listen to it too. If the frequency you're on goes quiet, that's the next one to try.`,
          `ELTs. The old analog kind broadcasts a swept tone on the emergency frequency. Satellites stopped listening for those in 2009, so now an analog ELT depends on someone nearby hearing it. The newer digital kind talks to the satellites, carries the owner's information, and has to be registered with NOAA, and search and rescue reacts to it much faster. Look at the label on Biscuit's and tell me which she has. Analog ELTs get tested only in the first 5 minutes after the hour, three sweeps at most, and never in the air. Before you shut down after a hard landing, or any landing, listen on the emergency frequency for a second to make sure yours didn't go off. 91.207 has the battery rules you met in May: replace it after an hour of cumulative use or half its useful life, and inspect the ELT every 12 calendar months.`,
          `And if you ever lose the ground at night, in a cloud or a snow shower, the AFH says the only concern is keeping the wings level. Fly the attitude indicator, gently, with your fingertips. Untrained pilots lose control in about 10 minutes on instruments alone. Tell ATC you aren't instrument rated and declare the emergency. The PHAK's advice for preventing it is the reason this chapter has the title it has: decide early to turn around and land at an alternate with better weather, and always consider safer alternatives rather than hope things work out.`],
        terms: [[`Urgency (AIM 6-1)`,`At least urgency the moment you become doubtful about position, fuel, weather, or safety.`],[`MAYDAY / PAN-PAN`,`Distress and urgency, preferably three times each.`],[`Codes`,`Keep your assigned code with ATC. 7700 if you can't establish contact. 7600 lost comm. 7500 hijack.`],[`ELT`,`Analog: emergency frequency tone, tests first 5 minutes after the hour, 3 sweeps. 406: satellites, NOAA registration.`],[`91.3(b)`,`PIC may deviate from rules to meet an emergency; report on request.`]],
        exam: `Urgency starts at doubt. Already talking to ATC, keep your code. Analog ELT tests in the first 5 minutes of the hour.` },
      { h: `Night fuel, and the night you don't go`,
        body: [`91.151(a)(2) is the night half of the fuel rule. You can't begin a VFR flight in an airplane unless, considering the wind and forecast weather, you have enough fuel to fly to your first point of intended landing and then fly at normal cruise for at least 45 minutes at night. Forty five instead of thirty, because at night the alternate is farther away in every sense. At 8 gallons an hour, that's 6 gallons. The club's land with an hour still applies. Walt has his own number, and he'll tell you.`,
          `91.103 already makes you think about alternatives before you leave. At night they shrink. A field you'd happily land in at noon is a dark rectangle at nine. An airport with lights on a radio switch is an airport where you need a radio. A cross-country at night is planned around lit airports, roads you can follow, and the highest terrain you'll cross, and the PHAK suggests flying the route by day first and deciding the lowest altitude you're willing to accept at night. If the weather won't let you hold that altitude, you turn around early.`,
          `The last thing in this chapter is the one the test asks about least and you'll use most. The AFH says the greatest worry in a single-engine airplane at night is an engine failure, and then it says, quietly, that continuing into adverse weather and poor judgment cause most of the serious accidents. The engine almost never quits. People almost always keep going. External pressure, a dinner, a family waiting, a deadline, is the thing you learned to name last month. The way you handle it is to decide what turns you around before you're in the car, and then to let it.`],
        terms: [[`91.151(a)(2)`,`Night: fuel to the first intended landing plus 45 minutes at normal cruise.`],[`Night alternates`,`Lit airports, followable roads, known terrain. Plan a minimum altitude and a turnaround.`],[`Most serious night accidents`,`AFH: continued flight into adverse weather and poor judgment, more than engine failure.`]],
        exam: `Night reserve is 45 minutes. Calculation questions give a burn and a time and ask for total fuel with the night reserve.` }
    ]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f10s1`, domain:`Navigation and emergencies`, task:`XI`, title:`Thirty minutes`, q:`Takeoff is just before twilight ends at 5:07. How should Jordan spend the time until then?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Wednesday, November 25, 4:15 PM, the ramp. Low sun on the foothills. The hangar shadows reached clear across the taxiway and onto the grass. Jordan had slept until one. Thursday at seven she was back at the station for Thanksgiving, which on an ambulance is a normal shift plus a pie somebody's mother drops off at the bay door around noon.`,`Abby came out of the office with two flashlights. One was the size of a baguette, with a red lens on a little hinge that flipped down over the white one. The other hung on a lanyard and smelled faintly of Abby's car, which was coffee and old fries.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Sunset's 4:38. Twilight ends 5:07. We take off before that and land after, which is the point of tonight. Six full stops. And the examiner is going to ask you what you did with the next half hour, so."</span>`,`She checked her watch. Looked at the sky. Looked at Jordan.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Preflight now while it's light, then sit in the dim cockpit.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:3,health:4},
          after:`She does the walkaround with the white light anyway, for the practice, while there's still daylight to check it against. Master on. Beacon, strobes, landing light. Red on the left wingtip, green on the right, white on the tail. Abby shows her the knuckle tap on each position light, and none of them so much as blink. Then she climbs in, turns the panel lights down to a dull orange, flips the red lens over, and sits. Abby sits. Nobody talks. Ten minutes in, the ramp starts coming back to her in grays, tie down rings, chocks, a gas cap somebody left sitting on a wing two rows over. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"There. Night eyes. Now don't look at your phone. I mean it. It's a flashlight with opinions."</span>`,
          why:`The AFH recommends checking every light during the preflight and tapping the position light fixtures. Full dark adaptation takes about 30 minutes, moderate adaptation about 20 under dim red light, and any bright light for a second or more costs some of it.`},
        {t:`Wait for full dark to do the preflight with her phone's flashlight.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Two problems. You'll miss things you could see right now. And that phone light is the brightest thing on this ramp. Every time you look at it, you start the half hour over."</span> Jordan looks at the wing, at the fuel caps catching the last of the sun. She does it now.`,
          why:`The handbooks recommend a careful preflight with a proper flashlight, preferably before dark when possible. A bright white phone light repeatedly destroys the dark adaptation that takes about 30 minutes to build.`},
        {t:`Preflight now while it's light, then wait in the FBO, coffee in hand.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`The preflight is good. Then she sits under the FBO's fluorescent lights for twenty minutes, and when she walks back out to the airplane the ramp is a black lake with orange dots in it. Abby lets her find the step by feel. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Good walkaround. Then you spent twenty minutes in a light box. Your eyes start over at the door."</span>`,
          why:`Doing the preflight in daylight is good practice, but bright indoor lighting resets dark adaptation. Spending the wait somewhere dim, like the cockpit with the panel lights down, preserves it.`},
        {t:`Skip the light check, and count Russ's Saturday flight as proof they work.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Russ flew her at noon. To a burger."</span> She waits. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Position lights are required from sunset to sunrise. That's twenty minutes from now. Go turn them on and walk around her."</span>`,
          why:`91.209 requires lighted position lights from sunset to sunrise, and 91.205(c) lists them as required night equipment. A daytime flight tells you nothing about whether the lights work. Check them yourself.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f10s2`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.H`, title:`Stars and ranch lights`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`First takeoff at 4:58, into a sky the color of a bruise. First landing at 5:05, technically a day landing, though it didn't feel like one. By the third takeoff it was night by the logbook's definition. Off three five right and right for the downwind, the runway slid back under the wing, and then there was nothing in the windshield at all.`,`East, out the left window, the subdivisions just stopped, like somebody had run out of houses. Past them was the plains, black, with ranch lights sprinkled on it here and there, and above the ranch lights, stars, and it was hard to say exactly where one bunch quit and the other started. The moon was coming up too. Huge, orange, ninety eight percent, sitting on the ground like a dropped plate.`,`Off the wing a string of headlights ran uphill across her window. She rolled level with it. Felt fine.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"How are your wings?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Find the moon on the horizon and level the wings against it.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`She finds the moon and squares the wings to the ground under it. Abby reaches over and taps the attitude indicator once with a fingernail. Right wing down, about a hand. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Moon's on a hill. Road's on a hill. Horizon's wherever this guy says."</span> Jordan levels it on the instrument, and for a long annoying minute the whole outside world looks crooked, until her head gives up and agrees.`,
          why:`At night, ground lights, stars, a rising moon, and sloping lines of lights can all create a false horizon (AIM 8-1-5). When the outside picture is ambiguous, the attitude indicator is the reference.`},
        {t:`Keep flying by the lights outside, with a check of the instruments every few seconds.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`She tries it. Out, in, out. Every time she comes back inside, the little airplane on the attitude indicator is somewhere new. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"You're arguing with it. Tonight it's the only instrument with a view. Let it drive. You look outside for traffic, not for level."</span>`,
          why:`Cross-checking is good, but when the outside references are misleading, splitting control between them and the instruments leads to the wings wandering. The AFH says to lean on the flight instruments at night, especially in climbs and turns.`},
        {t:`Level the wings on the attitude indicator and look outside only for traffic.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`Attitude indicator. Ten degrees of right bank she hadn't felt and hadn't asked for. She rolls level, checks 6,900 on the altimeter, and right away the airplane swears to her it's banking left. She lets it swear. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"That's the road. Road's on a hill. Classic false horizon, and you'll get it every single time you turn east out here."</span> Jordan says it out loud so it'll stick. <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Road's on a hill."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Abby" data-tone="Deadpan, dry, quietly approving.">"Get it printed on a mug."</span>`,
          why:`A dark scene with ground lights and stars, or a line of lights on sloping ground, can create a false horizon. The fix is the flight instruments for attitude, with the outside scan for traffic.`},
        {t:`Ask Abby to take the airplane for a minute while her eyes adjust.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"I could. And the first night you're up here by yourself and it happens, who are you giving it to?"</span> She taps the attitude indicator again. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Him. Give it to him."</span>`,
          why:`Asking for help is never wrong in a real emergency, but this is a predictable night illusion with a standard fix: transfer attitude control to the instruments. The skill being trained is recognizing it and doing that yourself.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f10s3`, domain:`Airport and flight`, task:`III.A`, title:`Four lights`, q:`On a night final, the PAPI shows Jordan slightly high and drifting higher. How should she correct?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Fourth landing. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"Niner One Echo, runway three five right, cleared to land."</span> She read it back. On base the approach lights were doing their thing, a little white rabbit running at the runway over and over, and the runway was a pair of white rows with a green bar at her end. Left side, a bit down, the PAPI.`,`Problem was, it looked right there. She'd hung on to her altitude on base an extra few seconds because of that, and now on final the PAPI said three white and one red. Then four white.`,`10,000 feet of runway. Abby had her chin on her fist and was looking out her side like she was thinking about lunch.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Lower the nose to get down to the glide path quickly, then level off on it.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`She shoves the nose over. Fifteen knots she didn't order show up on the airspeed, and the PAPI goes four white, two and two, four red, basically instantly, and now she's low and fast at once over the approach lights. Power in, pull. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Cool, two problems."</span> A beat. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Go around."</span> Jordan goes around. Second approach, nothing happens. Abby likes it when nothing happens.`,
          why:`Diving to correct a high approach builds airspeed and can overshoot into a low, fast approach. Small, early corrections of pitch and power keep the approach stabilized.`},
        {t:`Fly the runway by eye, and land a little long on the ten thousand feet.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`She lands long. Floats halfway down three five right while Abby sighs, audibly, the way you'd sigh at a printer. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"You were high because the lights are bright and the ground's dark. That's the illusion, that's the whole thing. That's why they bolted a PAPI to the grass. Use it."</span>`,
          why:`Bright runway and approach lights with dark surroundings make the runway appear closer, and pilots tend to fly high. The PAPI exists to give a reliable glide path when visual judgment is unreliable.`},
        {t:`Go around, and set up a better approach from the start.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Centennial Tower, Niner One Echo, going around."</span> Climb, crosswind, downwind, and this time she watches those four lights from the turn to final. Fine landing. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Never wrong. Pricey, for one light high on a mile final. Your left hand could've handled that."</span>`,
          why:`A go-around is always acceptable when an approach doesn't look right, and the AFH says to go around whenever position is in doubt. Here, though, she knew exactly where she was, and a small power reduction would have corrected it.`},
        {t:`Ease a little power off and hold 65 until she's back on the glide path.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Little high."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"So fix it."</span> She takes a hundred RPM out and leaves 65 where it is, and Biscuit sinks a bit, and the box goes three white and then two and two. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"There. Now quit touching it."</span> Jordan puts the hundred back. Then she waits for the landing light to find the tire marks. It takes forever. Black rubber, finally, smeared across the white, and she starts the round out. On the rollout Abby says, <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Bright runway, dark ground. Gets everybody. Got me at Grand Junction my first winter, and I had eight hundred hours."</span>`,
          why:`Three white and one red is slightly high on a four-light PAPI. A small power reduction at constant airspeed captures the glide path. AFH: round out when the landing light shows the tire marks on the runway.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f10s4`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.B`, title:`Tap the light`, q:`What's the right call on a flickering position light during a night preflight?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`${(G.nopersonalmins)?`After the first night she'd uncapped a pen over the card on the panel, meaning to add a night line. Then didn't. Abby's numbers in Jordan's handwriting. Adding to them felt like signing Abby's name. Pen back in the cup holder.`:`After the first night she'd added a line to the bottom of the card on the panel, smaller than the rest because she was out of room. NIGHT: DUAL ONLY. CEILING 5,000 AGL ALL THE WAY. NO SNOW. NO RIDGES UNDER A LOWERING DECK. IF THE LIGHTS GO FUZZY, TURN AROUND.`}`,`Thursday, December 3, 7:40 PM. Night two. She'd come off a shift at seven that morning and slept until two, and had decided, out loud, in her kitchen, that she was fine, and had then made herself answer every letter of IMSAFE before she believed it. No moon tonight. It wouldn't be up until almost three in the morning. Abby had picked the night on purpose.`,`It was minus six on the ramp. Light leaked out where the hangar door didn't quite meet the frame. Inside, somebody was singing with the radio, badly. Marisol, then, fixing something that wasn't broken.`,`On the walkaround, master on, Jordan tapped the red light on the left wingtip with one knuckle, the way she had the first night. It went out. She tapped it again. It came on. Again. Out. It stayed out.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Go, with the strobes and the beacon making her plenty visible.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`Abby keeps her eyes on the checklist, pen moving. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Read me 91.205(c)(2)."</span> Jordan doesn't need to read it. Approved position lights. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"91.209."</span> Lit, sunset to sunrise. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"The strobe says hey, airplane. It doesn't say which end is the front. Red and green do, and the guy coming at you is the one who needs that."</span>`,
          why:`Position lights are required equipment for night VFR under 91.205(c) and must be lit from sunset to sunrise under 91.209. Anticollision lights don't replace them, because only the position lights show direction.`},
        {t:`Placard it inoperative under 91.213, and stay in the pattern close to the airport.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"91.213 has a list of stuff you can't defer. Top of the list, anything 91.205 wants for the flight you're about to fly, and tonight that's a night flight."</span> Thumb over her shoulder at the hangar. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Also, there's an A and P forty feet away butchering Fleetwood Mac."</span>`,
          why:`91.213(d)(2)(iii) doesn't allow operation with inoperative equipment that 91.205 requires for the specific kind of operation. At night, position lights are required, so a placard doesn't make the airplane legal.`},
        {t:`Cancel the lesson and write the light up for Marisol in the morning.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`She gets as far as LH POS LT on the squawk sheet. Abby watches her spell it. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Correct. Legal airplane by tomorrow. Or."</span> She tilts her head at the hangar. The singing has hit the chorus and gotten worse. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Go ask her. She'll sulk for a week if you don't."</span>`,
          why:`Grounding the airplane for night until the required light is fixed is legal and safe. But a mechanic was available, and fixing it properly with a logbook entry was the better outcome.`},
        {t:`Ask Marisol to fix it and sign it off tonight, before they go.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`Marisol comes out in a headlamp with a spare bulb held in her teeth. The screwdriver comes out of a pocket Jordan didn't know the coveralls had. Two screws, lens off. The old bulb is scorched black on one side and the contact has gone fuzzy green. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"There's your blink."</span> A wire brush, a new bulb, and then Jordan has to knock on it ten times while Marisol counts out loud. It stays on. Then the logbook, under the hangar light: date, tach time, lamp replaced, socket cleaned, signature, and the pencil goes back behind her ear. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol" data-tone="Quiet, fond, plain.">"Danny tapped them too. Said the airplane would tell you things if you knocked."</span>`,
          why:`The AFH recommends tapping position light fixtures during the night preflight and finding the cause of any flicker before flight. Position lights are required for night VFR, so the fix and the maintenance record come before takeoff.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f10s5`, domain:`Navigation and emergencies`, task:`IX`, title:`Sixty five`, q:`With the power at idle at night over unlit fields, where should Jordan point Biscuit?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`8:31 PM, the practice area just north of Bennett, about twenty miles northeast of Centennial, 7,500 feet. The ground out here was about 5,500 feet and almost entirely invisible. There were the lights of Bennett off to the south, a gas station on I-70, and a county road running north and south below them with a farm light at every mile like beads. Between the beads, nothing. Fields, presumably.`,`Abby had shown her the winds before they left: about 15 knots out of the north at this altitude. She'd also, earlier, made Jordan read the glide card taped inside the POH cover, Marisol's capitals on an index card: <b>BEST GLIDE 65 KIAS. FLAPS UP. 1.5 NM PER 1,000 FT AGL, NO WIND.</b>`,`Abby reached over and pulled the throttle to idle.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Engine failure. Talk to me. We stop at 6,500, a thousand above the ground, and go home."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Pitch for 65 and turn toward the lights of Bennett to see what's below.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`She turns south. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Into houses. And streets. With wires on them. The handbook says turn away from congested areas."</span> And downwind, she realizes, looking at the groundspeed. She turns back north, and she's lost four hundred feet doing it.`,
          why:`The AFH's night engine failure guidance is to turn toward an airport or away from congested areas, and to plan for an unlighted part of known terrain near public access. Towns have structures and wires.`},
        {t:`Pitch for 65 and turn toward the darkest field, the one farthest from anything.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`She finds it, a big dark nothing a mile east. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Dark is right. How long until somebody finds you there?"</span> Jordan looks at it. No road for two miles. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"The book says near public access. Dark field, lit road. You get both."</span>`,
          why:`An unlighted area is correct, since lights mean buildings and people. But the AFH also recommends a landing area close to public access to make rescue easier.`},
        {t:`Pitch for 65 and turn for the dark field beside the lit county road.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`Nose up, trim, 65. She puts the field off the nose, a dark rectangle a mile and change north with a farm light at its corner and the road running down its west side. Into the wind. <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Fuel selector both, mixture rich, carb heat on, mags both, primer in and locked."</span> Nothing changes, because Abby's hand is on the throttle. <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Mayday, mayday, mayday, Cessna Four Seven Niner One Echo, engine failure, twenty miles northeast of Centennial, landing in a field."</span> She says it to the windshield, not the radio. Landing light on at 6,800 to test it. The beam goes out and finds nothing. At 6,500 Abby pushes the throttle in and the engine roars back like it was offended. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"You'd have made it. You'd have seen the stubble in time from about two hundred feet. That's all a landing light gives you."</span>`,
          why:`AFH night engine failure: best glide, toward an unlighted area of known terrain near public access, into the wind, check the causes, announce the emergency, and use the landing light in time to see the terrain. Avoid the road itself, which may have wires.`},
        {t:`Pitch for 65 and head straight for Centennial, nose up a bit to stretch it.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Twenty miles. You've got about three."</span> She points at the airspeed, which is dropping toward 55 as Jordan holds the nose up. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"And slower than best glide doesn't get you farther. It gets you lower, sooner, closer to a stall."</span>`,
          why:`From 2,000 feet AGL, Biscuit glides about 3 NM in still air, less into a headwind. Raising the nose below best glide speed reduces distance and approaches the stall. Pick a field within reach.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f10s6`, domain:`Airport and flight`, task:`III.A`, title:`Seven clicks`, q:`Eight miles out, the runway is still black. How should Jordan bring up the lights?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`9:08 PM. The Space Port's tower had closed at nine, and the airport sat out on the plains ahead of them inside the Bravo's notch, under the 70, its beacon turning white and green, white and green, and below it nothing. No runway. A black hole where an eight thousand foot runway was supposed to be.`,`Jordan had the Chart Supplement entry on her kneeboard, copied the night before. The runway lights were on a radio switch now, on the airport's common frequency, which she'd already tuned. She'd announced herself on it, eight miles east, inbound for runway 26.`,`Abby had her hands in her lap.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Key the mic seven times in five seconds, then check the lights came on.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`Click click click click click click click. The runway comes on all at once, two long lines of white drawn on the dark, the threshold green, and a pair of lights to the left of the near end, one red, one white. <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Two-light PAPI. One and one is on path."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Write down the time."</span> 9:08. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Fifteen minutes from now they go out whether you're on final or not. Key them again on downwind."</span> She does, at 9:15, and again on the next downwind, without being told.`,
          why:`AIM 2-1-8: key the microphone 7 times within 5 seconds to turn on all controlled lights at maximum intensity, adjust lower with 5 or 3 if desired. Lights stay on 15 minutes from the last activation, so key again near the field.`},
        {t:`Key the mic three times for the lowest setting on the runway lights.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Three clicks. A dim set of edge lights, barely there against the dark. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Seven first. Always. Some airports, three won't turn on everything. Get it all on, then turn it down with five or three if it's too bright."</span> She keys seven, and the runway jumps out of the ground.`,
          why:`The AIM recommends always keying 7 times initially so all controlled lighting comes on at maximum, then adjusting down with 5 or 3 clicks where the system allows.`},
        {t:`Land on the landing light and the GPS, with eight thousand feet ahead.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`Abby turns her head and looks at her, first time all night. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"So. A runway you can't see, at an airport you can't see, using a light that won't hit the ground till you're two hundred feet up."</span> Jordan keys seven.`,
          why:`A landing light doesn't illuminate the runway environment from pattern altitude, and GPS isn't a landing aid for a VFR approach to an unlit runway. Activate the runway lighting.`},
        {t:`Call the tower on its frequency and ask them to turn the runway lights on.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-1,health:0},
          after:`She calls. Nothing. It's 9:08 and the tower went home at nine. Somebody else on the frequency, some pilot parked or passing, says <span class="said" data-who="man">"Tower's closed. Seven clicks."</span> Jordan says thanks and clicks.`,
          why:`After the tower closes, the airport's lighting is pilot controlled on the published frequency. Know the airport's hours and lighting from the Chart Supplement before arriving.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f10s7`, domain:`Navigation and emergencies`, task:`IX`, title:`Discharge`, q:`The ammeter shows a steady discharge on the climb home at night. What should Jordan do?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`9:36 PM. Three miles west of the Space Port, climbing for home under the 70. Behind them the runway lights were still on, burning down the last of their fifteen minutes. Abby reached across and flipped the ALT half of the master off.`,`The ammeter needle slid left of center and stayed there, a steady discharge.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Alternator's dead. You're on the battery. Go."</span>`,`Back in May, on a Saturday, with the cowling off and Marisol not looking up from her wire, Jordan had answered this exact question, a night flight over Limon. Easier with your feet on concrete.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Shed load and keep heading home to Centennial, twelve minutes away.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Landing light off, GPS off, second radio off, and she keeps pointing west. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Probably fine. If the battery's new. Biscuit's is three years old, and you've got no idea how long that needle was over there before you looked. Meanwhile the airport behind you is lit up right now."</span> Jordan turns around.`,
          why:`Shedding load is right. But the AFH says to plan to land at the nearest suitable airport, because battery life is uncertain, especially on an older battery. At night, a lit airport three miles away beats one twelve minutes away.`},
        {t:`Turn the master off to save the battery, and fly home by flashlight.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Okay, now there's no radio, no transponder, and no way to click on the lights at the next airport. You saved the electricity for an emergency. You're in it."</span>`,
          why:`Turning off the master removes radios, the transponder, lighting, and the ability to activate pilot controlled lighting. The AFH says shed nonessential loads, notify ATC, and land at the nearest suitable airport.`},
        {t:`Shed load and turn back to the Space Port while it's still lit.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`Landing light off. GPS off. The second radio off. Panel lights down to the least she can read. She keeps one radio and the transponder. <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Denver Approach, Niner One Echo, alternator failure, returning to the Space Port, three miles west."</span> She banks back east and the runway is right there, still lit, a minute or two left on its timer. She keys it again, seven clicks, on downwind. Abby flips the ALT back on as they turn final, because it's training and the landing light makes the landing easier to grade. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Nearest suitable. At night that means lit. You picked the one you'd just keyed. That's the whole lesson."</span>`,
          why:`AFH: after a generating system failure, immediately shed nonessential loads, notify ATC, and plan to land at the nearest suitable airport. Keep what you need for communication and the landing, including what it takes to activate the lights.`},
        {t:`Pull the circuit breakers one at a time to find the bad circuit.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`She reaches for the breaker panel in the dark. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"It's an alternator, not a short. You're spending battery and attention looking for a fault you already know about. Shed, talk, land."</span>`,
          why:`A steady discharge on the ammeter means the alternator isn't carrying the load. Troubleshooting breakers costs time and battery. Shed load, notify ATC, and land at the nearest suitable airport.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f10s8`, domain:`Navigation and emergencies`, task:`IX`, title:`Fuel off`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Monday, December 7, eleven in the morning, the hangar. Biscuit's cowling was off again. Marisol called it an oil seep. Walt, walking through with a coffee, called it a hobby. Jordan's logbook said 3.6 hours of night now and ten night landings, five from the Wednesday before Thanksgiving and five from last Thursday. One box left. The cross-country.`,`Marisol had the little red extinguisher out from under the left seat and was reading it. The gauge, the date, the fine print, all of it, the way some people read a cereal box at breakfast.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Pop quiz. Night, 7,500, out over the plains. You smell something hot. Then smoke out the bottom of the cowling, right here,"</span> and she pointed the extinguisher at the bottom of the engine, at the exhaust pipes and the fuel lines, <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"and there's light under it, and it's not your landing light. It's orange. What do your hands do first?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Master switch off, cutting all electrical power in the airplane.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Some checklists get there, eventually. But if it's the engine on fire, electricity isn't what's feeding it."</span> She flicks the fuel line with a fingernail. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Gas is. Fuel first. Master's on Biscuit's list, third, and when it goes off so do the radio and the transponder. Mayday before that, or nobody knows which field you picked."</span>`,
          why:`The AFH notes that some checklists include the master switch, but unless the fire is electrical or touchdown is imminent, turning it off removes radios and the transponder. For an engine fire, the first step is cutting off fuel.`},
        {t:`Open the windows and vents to clear the smoke.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-4},
          after:`Marisol wrinkles her nose. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Great. Now it's got air and you've got smoke in your lap. Vents are later. Fire first, extinguisher first if it's in the cabin, then vents. Smoke gets worse when you crack them? Shut them."</span>`,
          why:`The AFH says cabin vents should be opened to clear smoke only after the fire is controlled and the extinguisher has been used, and closed if the smoke increases. Opening them first can feed the fire.`},
        {t:`Dive steeply at high speed to blow the fire out.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Going fast does blow some of them out. It also pulls off a wing that's been cooking for a minute, and the book mentions that part."</span> She slides the extinguisher back under the seat. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Fuel off. Then come down at a speed that doesn't break anything that's already hot."</span>`,
          why:`The AFH notes a high-speed descent could blow out a fire, but fire may already have weakened the structure, and the airspeed limits still apply. The first action is shutting off fuel, then an emergency descent and forced landing.`},
        {t:`Mixture to idle cutoff and the fuel selector to off.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Yep."</span> She reaches in and pulls the mixture all the way out to show her, then puts a boot on the fuel selector on the floor, the sticky one. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"All the way to OFF, and feel it seat. She's the one that hangs up short of BOTH, so don't trust your hand, trust the click. Do it blind a few times. Mags? The AFH says leave them on so the engine burns off what's in the lines. Biscuit's checklist doesn't mention them. If the fire goes out, it stays out. No restart. Biscuit's checklist says master off third, so make your mayday before you get there. Then you're a glider, with a story."</span> She sets the extinguisher on the wing. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"This one's for the cabin. Pin, aim low at the base, squeeze, sweep. Then crack a vent, because the stuff in there isn't great to breathe either."</span>`,
          why:`AFH engine fire procedure, unless the POH directs otherwise: shut off fuel with the mixture at idle cutoff and the fuel selector off, leave the ignition on to burn the fuel in the lines, don't restart, and make a forced landing.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f10s9`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.D`, title:`The number you don't plan to`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Thursday, December 10, four in the afternoon, the briefing room. The Pueblo nav log was done, two pages in pencil, and Walt had asked to see it, which Walt didn't do.`,`${(G.thinfuel)?`He put a sheet of paper on the table before he sat down. It was the club fuel log from November 22, with her Space Port receipt stapled to it, and nothing stapled for Greeley. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"We said we'd talk. This is the talk."</span> He didn't say it unkindly. He said it like a man who'd been saving it, which he had. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"You landed at the Space Port with legal and a little. Now you want to do it at night, over the Divide, in December. So let's see what you land with this time."</span>`:`He'd read her November fuel column once already and initialed it. He read this one the way he'd read release paperwork for thirty years, one finger moving down the page, glasses on the end of his nose. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Night's different. You know the rule. I want to hear what you plan."</span>`}`,`Her log said 80 miles each way by I-25, 118 knots south with the forecast tailwind and 92 coming home into it, 8 gallons an hour, a gallon for taxi and a gallon for the climb at each end. Biscuit's tanks held 40 usable.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Walt">"What do you land with? And where's your alternate on the way home?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Plan the 30 minute day reserve, going by a takeoff before full dark.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`Walt takes his glasses off. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"You take off at 5:15. Twilight ends at 5:06. And you land in Pueblo at six, in the dark, which is where the reserve gets used. You want to argue minutes with the regulation, or you want 45?"</span> She writes 45.`,
          why:`91.151(a)(2) requires 45 minutes at night. This departure is after the end of civil twilight, and the whole flight and its arrival are at night. When in any doubt, plan to the more conservative reserve.`},
        {t:`Fill the tanks and plan 45 minutes, with no alternate for the way home.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Fuel is never the problem until it's the only problem."</span> He taps the I-25 line on her chart. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Coming home, the Divide's in front of you. Say it's down. Where do you go, and do you have the gas to get there and still have 45?"</span> Jordan starts writing.`,
          why:`91.103 requires knowing the alternatives available if the planned flight can't be completed. Full tanks help, but a fuel plan includes where you'd go if the destination isn't reachable and the fuel to get there with the reserve.`},
        {t:`Leave with half tanks, and top off at Pueblo after dinner.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Legal. Twenty gets you there with plenty. Then you're counting on a card reader at a pump in the dark at 8:30 on a Sunday."</span> He doesn't say Russ. He doesn't have to. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Weight's not your problem with two people in December. Fill it here."</span>`,
          why:`Planning to refuel at the destination is legal, but it adds a dependency on fuel availability at night. With light loading and cold temperatures, departing with full tanks removes that risk.`},
        {t:`Full tanks, and a 45 minute reserve even after a turnaround at Monument.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`She shows him the bottom of the page, where she'd already done it. Full tanks, 40. Pueblo with about 32. Home with about 23 if nothing goes wrong. And the line she'd written for him, because she knew he'd ask: if the Divide is down coming home, Pueblo to Monument and back to Pueblo, plus 45, is about 16 gallons, and she'd leave Pueblo with 32. Walt reads it twice. Then he takes a pen out of his jacket and, under her numbers, in small square capitals, writes <b>RELEASED. W.H.</b> <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Old habit. That's the number I'd have wanted on a release."</span>`,
          why:`A dispatcher's fuel plan covers the destination, an alternate, and the reserve. Planning the return so a turnaround at the worst point still leaves the 91.151 night reserve is the ACS fuel planning risk element done properly.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f10s10`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.C`, title:`FM140400`, q:`The TAF brings snow and a 2,500 foot overcast at nine tonight, on her return. How should Jordan decide?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Sunday, December 13, 8:50 in the morning, her kitchen. Her mother's birthday. She'd texted happy birthday at 7:01 and gotten back <b>Thank you mija. Drive safe if you drive.</b>, which was her mother's way of saying a great many things.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Weather, printed at 8:44 AM"><code>METAR KAPA 131453Z 34006KT 10SM SCT120 M03/M11 A3024 RMK AO2<br><br>TAF KAPA 131120Z 1312/1412 34008KT P6SM SCT120<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;FM132100 36010KT P6SM BKN080<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;FM140400 03012KT 4SM -SN BR OVC025<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;FM140900 03010KT 2SM -SN BR OVC012<br><br>TAF KPUB 131120Z 1312/1412 32008KT P6SM FEW120<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;FM140200 34010KT P6SM SCT080<br><br>FB DEN 9000 3413-08<br><br>NOTES OFF THE GFA GRAPHIC, FRONT RANGE AND PALMER DIVIDE: UPSLOPE DEVELOPING THIS EVENING BEHIND A COLD FRONT. CIG LWRG TO 1000-2500 AGL AND -SN, EARLIEST OVER THE PALMER DIVIDE. TIMING UNCERTAIN.</code></prop>`,`She did the conversion out loud. Zero four hundred Zulu on the 14th, minus seven, is nine o'clock tonight. Her plan had her leaving Pueblo at 8:40 and landing at Centennial at about 9:35. Right into it.`,`And the graphical forecast said the Divide went first. Timing uncertain.`,`${(G.nopersonalmins)?`The only ceiling on the card over the mag switch was Abby's, 3,000 AGL, written for solo days in the pattern. It was still the only number she had.`:`The night line on the card over the mag switch said 5,000 AGL all the way. She'd written it after the first night, when nothing was riding on it.`}`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Keep the plan, but leave Pueblo by eight to be home before nine.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-1,health:-4},
          after:`She texts the plan to Abby, who calls. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"So the plan is to beat it."</span> Jordan says yes. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"The forecast says the Divide goes first and nobody knows when. You're planning on the TAF being right to the hour, at night, about the one thing it says it's unsure of. Who else moved the time earlier to beat the weather?"</span> Jordan doesn't answer, and doesn't need to. She knows from Claire.`,
          why:`Moving a departure earlier to beat forecast weather depends on the forecast being exact, and the GFA explicitly called the timing uncertain. It's the go fever pattern from chapter 8: shrinking the margin instead of keeping it.`},
        {t:`Make the call at quarter to five on the newest weather, car as plan B.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`She writes it on the nav log in red before she can talk herself out of it. <b>4:45 PM, NEWEST TAF, METARS, PIREPS. DIVIDE CEILING PER THE CARD, ALL THE WAY, BOTH WAYS. IF NOT, I DRIVE. LEAVE BY 5:15 EITHER WAY.</b> She takes a picture and sends it to Abby, who sends back a thumbs up and then, a minute later, <b>Also put the cake in your car now. Either way.</b>`,
          why:`The PHAK and RMH recommend setting decision criteria before the pressure arrives. A decision point on the latest data, a specific weather test for the critical terrain, and a ready alternative are what make a no-go easy to carry out.`},
        {t:`Make the call now and cancel the flight while the day's still open.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:2},
          after:`Abby, on the phone: <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"I'll take a cancel from you any day of the week. But that TAF's from before five this morning and you're deciding a nine o'clock flight off it. Could come early. Could hold off till midnight. So pick a time, decide then on whatever's newest, and keep the car warm."</span>`,
          why:`Canceling is always a safe choice. But a forecast this far ahead, with uncertain timing, is better used to set a decision point and criteria than to decide outright. Risk management includes getting the best information before you choose.`},
        {t:`Fly down, and leave Biscuit in Pueblo if the return looks bad.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"That's an out, and I like that you have one. It's an out for the way home."</span> She lets it sit. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"The GFA says the Divide goes first. You cross the Divide at 5:30 going down. What's the out for that?"</span>`,
          why:`Leaving the airplane at the destination is a reasonable alternative for the return. But the forecast hazard, lowering ceilings over high terrain with uncertain timing, also affects the outbound leg, and the plan needs a decision point for that too.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f10s11`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.H`, title:`Face up on the table`, q:`Jordan's family is expecting her in Pueblo, and the weather's uncertain. What should she tell them?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`1:40 PM, and her mother called. Called, not texted. Jordan watched the screen through two rings before she picked up.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Lourdes" data-tone="Quiet, careful, holding it together.">"Claire says you're going to fly to my dinner."</span> A pause. <span class="said" data-who="Lourdes" data-tone="Quiet, careful, holding it together.">"Your brother always flew. Every year I sat in the back room at Velarde's with my phone on the table, face up, next to the bread, and every year the waiters knew not to move it."</span> Another pause, longer. <span class="said" data-who="Lourdes" data-tone="Quiet, careful, holding it together.">"I'm not telling you not to come. I want you there. I'm telling you about the phone."</span>`,`Behind her mother's voice she could hear Claire's kitchen, a cabinet banging, Ellie somewhere asking something about whether there'd be the flan with the burnt part. They were leaving at three. Ellie had made a sign. Jordan knew about the sign because Ellie had sent her a picture of it at noon: WELCOME TO PUEBLO AUNT JORDAN, in purple, with an airplane on it that was clearly Biscuit, because the 4 in the tail number was backward.`,`Claire had texted underneath the picture. <b>She wants to watch you land. I told her maybe.</b>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Tell her mother now that she might drive, and text Claire the same.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Ma. I might drive. Weather's coming over the Divide tonight, and I decide at quarter to five. Either way I'm there. If I drive I'm late, and I'll call when I leave."</span> The line goes quiet. Then: <span class="said" data-who="Lourdes">"Okay."</span> Then, in a voice Jordan hasn't heard out of her since high school: <span class="said" data-who="Lourdes" data-tone="Small, soft, close to tears.">"Your brother never once told me maybe."</span> Same text to Claire, plus: tell El the sign works for a car. Claire: <b>Told her. She's adding a car. It has like nine wheels.</b>`,
          why:`The PHAK lists managing passengers' and other people's expectations as a key way to manage external pressure. Telling the people waiting ahead of time that she might be late or drive takes away the pressure to make the flight happen.`},
        {t:`Promise Ellie she'll get to watch Biscuit land at six.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-1,health:-4},
          after:`She types it. Then she looks at it sitting there under the picture of the sign with the backward 4, and deletes it. Types it again: <b>Maybe. If the weather says yes.</b> Even the maybe feels like something she'll have to haul over the Divide with her.`,
          why:`Promising a specific arrival to someone waiting creates exactly the external pressure the PHAK calls the single most important thing to manage. It turns a weather decision into a promise to a child.`},
        {t:`Leave the weather out of it with her mother for now.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`She says see you at 6:30. Her mother says okay. She hangs up. Sits there. Takes her a minute to see it: that was the Danny move. Tell the worrier nothing, and then you have to make the flight happen, because now it's a secret too. She calls back. Her mother answers on the first ring. Her mother never answers on the first ring.`,
          why:`Hiding the uncertainty from the people waiting keeps their expectations fixed and adds pressure to complete the flight. Telling them early is how you keep a no-go available.`},
        {t:`Tell her mother she's driving, period, and get the call over with.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Lourdes">"Good."</span> A breath. <span class="said" data-who="Lourdes">"Is this because of me?"</span> Jordan says no. Not totally sure. Afterward she looks at the red 4:45 on her nav log and does the math. She just decided at 1:40 the thing she'd sworn she'd decide at 4:45. She texts Abby. Abby: <b>Allowed. Was it the weather or the phone call?</b>`,
          why:`Deciding to drive is always safe. But making the call early to end an uncomfortable conversation lets external pressure make the decision in the other direction. The plan was to decide on the newest weather.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f10s12`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.H`, title:`The Divide`, q:`A PIREP shows ceilings dropping fast over the Palmer Divide, and Centennial's TAF was amended. What should Jordan do?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`4:45 PM. Biscuit's left seat, at the tie down, preflight done in the last of the light, every light tapped twice, the tanks topped to the filler necks. Abby in the right seat with the iPad on her knee. Overhead, the overcast was high and still gray-white. To the south, over the Palmer Divide, it wasn't. It was a flat gray lid sitting low on the ridgeline, and the ridgeline was gone under it in places.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Weather, printed at 4:41 PM"><code>METAR KAPA 132253Z 36010KT 10SM BKN050 OVC070 M02/M08 A3027 RMK AO2<br><br>METAR KCOS 132254Z 01012KT 5SM -SN BR OVC028 M03/M05 A3028<br><br>METAR KPUB 132253Z 33009KT 10SM SCT080 01/M07 A3022<br><br>TAF KAPA 132320Z AMD 1323/1418 36010KT P6SM BKN050<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;FM140100 03012KT 4SM -SN BR OVC020<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;FM140400 03012KT 1 1/2SM -SN BR OVC008<br><br>UA /OV BRK360010/TM 2320/FL085/TP C182/SK OVC100/WX FV04SM -SN/RM CIG LWRG RAPIDLY. TERRAIN OBSC E OF I-25.</code></prop>`,`She did the arithmetic out loud, the way she'd do a dose. The amended TAF brought the lowering at Centennial from nine o'clock to six. The PIREP was from a Skylane twenty five minutes ago, ten miles north of the Black Forest VOR, right over the top of the Divide: overcast at 10,000, flight visibility four in light snow, ceiling lowering rapidly. The ground under that airplane was about 7,500 feet. Twenty five hundred feet between the ridge and the clouds, and getting smaller. Colorado Springs had a 2,800 foot overcast and snow. Pueblo was fine. Pueblo was always going to be fine.`,`${(G.nopersonalmins)?`She looked at the card above the mag switch. STUDENT SOLO LIMITS. CEILING 3,000 AGL. VISIBILITY 5 SM. NO PASSENGERS. Abby's numbers, in her own writing, made for a day in the pattern, and none of it was written for tonight. Except the ceiling line, which read straight onto the gap. Twenty five hundred between the ridge and the clouds. The card said three thousand. That was all the panel had to lean on. Whatever she decided, she was going to decide it right here, fresh, in the parking lot, the way Abby had told her not to.`:`She looked at the card above the mag switch, at the small line she'd squeezed in at the bottom after the first night. CEILING 5,000 AGL ALL THE WAY. NO SNOW. NO RIDGES UNDER A LOWERING DECK. And above it, in bigger letters, the line she'd written in November. I DON'T CHANGE THIS CARD IN THE PARKING LOT.`}`,`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Your call tonight,"</span> Abby said. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"It's legal right now, every piece of it. I'll fly what you decide. I'll tell you afterward what I'd have done."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Go now, while Centennial is VFR and the lowering is an hour out.`, s:0, flag:`pushedweather`, d:{trust:-4,conf:-2,health:-6},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Okay."</span> That's all she says. They're off at 5:12. By Castle Rock the overcast is right on top of them and the I-25 headlights below are a river with no banks. Over Larkspur the lights of the highway grow halos, then go soft, then go. Snow in the landing light, coming straight at the windshield like stars in a movie. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"My airplane."</span> Abby's voice is completely flat. Wings level on the attitude indicator, a slow standard rate turn, no more than fifteen degrees of bank, a hundred and eighty degrees that take a year. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Denver Approach, Niner One Echo, inadvertent IMC, turning north, request vectors to Centennial."</span> The controller gives them a heading and an altitude and says the words <span class="said" data-who="man" data-tone="Very calm, warm, unhurried.">"no traffic between you and the airport"</span> in a voice so calm Jordan wants to marry it. The lights of Castle Rock come back under the wing. They land on three five right at 5:51 in light snow, and Abby sits with her hands in her lap for a long time before she says anything.`,
          why:`The PIREP showed ceilings lowering rapidly over higher terrain at night, and the amended TAF moved the change earlier. The AFH warns that the first sign of cloud at night is ground lights fading, and the PHAK says decide early to turn around. Legal at departure isn't the same as safe for the route.`},
        {t:`Fly the east side of the Divide instead, out by Kiowa and Calhan.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-4},
          after:`She starts to brief it. Abby doesn't overrule her. She just asks. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Where's the PIREP?"</span> Jordan looks. Ten miles north of Black Forest. East of I-25. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"And the ground out there?"</span> The Black Forest is 7,500 feet too. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"And coming home at nine, Centennial is?"</span> Eight hundred overcast, by the TAF. Jordan puts the iPad down.`,
          why:`The PIREP was over the eastern side of the Divide, where the terrain is just as high. Upslope weather covers the whole area, and the return would arrive into forecast IFR at Centennial. A different route through the same weather isn't an alternative.`},
        {t:`Wait at the FBO until the six o'clock METARs, and decide then.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`She waits. At 5:53 the METARs update: Centennial broken at 2,500 in light snow, Colorado Springs overcast at 1,800. Abby turns the iPad around without a word. Jordan already knows. She's lost an hour, the road over Monument Hill is now in the snow too, and she'll be late to her mother's dinner by more than an hour instead of a little.`,
          why:`Waiting for more information can be reasonable, but here the trend was clear: an amended TAF moving the change earlier and a PIREP of rapidly lowering ceilings. Waiting for weather that is forecast to get worse mostly costs time and options, including the safe drive.`},
        {t:`Drive, and leave now before the snow reaches Monument Hill.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:6,conf:5,health:6},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Jordan" data-tone="Quiet, certain, deciding out loud.">"No."</span> It comes out easier than she expected, and quieter. <span class="said" data-who="Jordan" data-tone="Quiet, certain, deciding out loud.">"The Divide's at 2,500 and dropping. That's not a number I'm crossing at night. I'm driving."</span> Abby nods once, short, the nod she saves for a good checklist, and starts unbuckling. <span class="said" data-who="Abby" data-tone="Quiet, plain, approving.">"I'd have said no at four. I wanted to hear you say it."</span> Jordan calls Claire from the cockpit, and Claire says <span class="said" data-who="Claire">"Okay. I'll tell her. Drive safe, the pass is getting bad,"</span> and somewhere behind her Ellie shouts something about the car part of the sign.`,
          why:`The PIREP showed ceilings lowering rapidly over the highest terrain on the route, the TAF had been amended earlier, and it was night. The AFH says night VFR belongs in unrestricted visibility. Deciding on the ground, with a ready alternative, is the decision the whole chapter has been building.`}
      ]}
  ],
  drills:[
    {after:`f10s3`, title:`When it's night, and which night`,
     setup:[`Wednesday, November 25, at Centennial. From the Naval Observatory: sunset 4:38 PM, end of evening civil twilight 5:07 PM.`,`Jordan's flight: Biscuit started moving under its own power at 4:52 PM. Takeoffs at 4:58, 5:10, 5:24, 5:40, 5:54, and 6:08 PM. Full-stop landings at 5:05, 5:19, 5:33, 5:47, 6:01, and 6:15 PM, each after a trip around the pattern. Biscuit came to rest at the tie down at 6:22 PM.`,`Flight time runs from when the airplane moves under its own power for flight to when it comes to rest after landing (61.1). Night under 1.1 starts at the end of evening civil twilight. The 61.57(b) period starts 1 hour after sunset. Enter clock times without the colon, so 5:38 is 538.`],
     fields:[{label:`Total flight time (hours)`,answer:1.5,tol:0.05},{label:`Night flight time under 1.1 (minutes)`,answer:75,tol:0},{label:`Night full-stop landings toward 61.109's ten`,answer:5,tol:0},{label:`Time the 61.57(b) period begins`,answer:538,tol:0},{label:`Takeoff and landing pairs that would count toward 61.57(b) night currency`,answer:3,tol:0},{label:`Night full-stop landings still needed for 61.109`,answer:5,tol:0}],
     solution:[`Total: 4:52 to 6:22 is 90 minutes, 1.5 hours.`,`Night under 1.1 begins at 5:07, the end of civil twilight. 5:07 to 6:22 is 75 minutes, 1.25 hours, which she logs as 1.3 of her 1.5.`,`Landings: the 5:05 landing is before 5:07, so it's a day landing. The other five, 5:19 through 6:15, are night landings. 61.109(a)(2)(ii) needs ten. She has five.`,`61.57(b): sunset 4:38 plus 1 hour is 5:38, and the rule wants the takeoffs and the landings inside that period. Takeoffs at 5:40, 5:54, 6:08, landings at 5:47, 6:01, 6:15. The 5:33 landing misses by five minutes. Three pairs, which is exactly the number 61.57(b) asks for, if she were a certificated pilot wanting to carry passengers at night.`,`Notice the three different nights: position lights on at 4:38 (91.209), logging night from 5:07 (1.1), passenger currency landings from 5:38 (61.57(b)).`]},
    {after:`f10s5`, title:`How far she glides`,
     setup:[`Over the practice area at 7,500 feet MSL. The ground is 5,500 feet. Wind at that altitude, from the north at 15 knots.`,`Biscuit's glide card: best glide 65 KIAS, flaps up, 1.5 NM per 1,000 feet above the ground in still air (about 9 to 1). At these altitudes and temperatures, 65 knots indicated is about 72 knots true.`,`Wind changes the distance by changing the groundspeed, not the time in the air. Glide time = still-air distance ÷ TAS. Distance with wind = groundspeed × glide time.`],
     fields:[{label:`Height above the ground (feet)`,answer:2000,tol:0},{label:`Still-air glide distance (NM)`,answer:3,tol:0.01},{label:`Time in the air (minutes)`,answer:2.5,tol:0.05},{label:`Descent rate (feet per minute)`,answer:800,tol:10},{label:`Glide distance gliding north, into the wind (NM)`,answer:2.375,tol:0.03},{label:`Glide distance gliding south, downwind (NM)`,answer:3.625,tol:0.03},{label:`Margin to the field 1.2 NM north (NM)`,answer:1.175,tol:0.03}],
     solution:[`Height: 7,500 minus 5,500 = 2,000 feet AGL.`,`Still air: 2.0 × 1.5 = 3.0 NM.`,`Time: 3.0 NM ÷ 72 knots = 0.0417 hours × 60 = 2.5 minutes. Descent: 2,000 ÷ 2.5 = 800 feet per minute. Check against 9 to 1: 72 knots is about 7,290 feet per minute over the ground, and 7,290 ÷ 9 is 810. Close enough.`,`Into the wind: groundspeed 72 minus 15 = 57 knots. 57 × 2.5 ÷ 60 = 2.375 NM. Downwind: 72 + 15 = 87 knots. 87 × 2.5 ÷ 60 = 3.625 NM.`,`The field is 1.2 NM north, into the wind. 2.375 minus 1.2 = 1.175 NM to spare, which is room for a pattern. Downwind gets more distance and a faster touchdown, and doubling groundspeed quadruples the energy. Into the wind is the better landing.`]},
    {after:`f10s9`, title:`Fuel for Pueblo, the dispatcher's way`,
     setup:[`80 NM each way by I-25. TAS 105, winds at 9,000 from 340 true at 13: groundspeed 118 southbound, 92 northbound. Burn 8.0 gallons per hour. Each departure adds 1.0 gallon for start, taxi, and runup and 1.0 gallon for the climb. 40 gallons usable with full tanks.`,`91.151(a)(2): fuel to the first point of intended landing plus 45 minutes at normal cruise, at night.`,`Walt's alternate case: coming home, the Divide is down at Monument, 51.2 NM north of Pueblo. Fly from Pueblo to Monument at 92 knots, turn around, fly back to Pueblo at 118 knots, and still land with 45 minutes.`],
     fields:[{label:`Minutes, Centennial to Pueblo`,answer:40.7,tol:0.2},{label:`Fuel for that leg, with taxi and climb (gallons)`,answer:7.4,tol:0.1},{label:`Night reserve at 8.0 gal/hr (gallons)`,answer:6,tol:0},{label:`Legal minimum on board to leave Centennial (gallons)`,answer:13.4,tol:0.1},{label:`Minutes, Pueblo to Centennial`,answer:52.2,tol:0.2},{label:`Fuel for the return leg, with taxi and climb (gallons)`,answer:9,tol:0.1},{label:`Walt's number: Pueblo to Monument and back, plus 45 (gallons)`,answer:15.9,tol:0.1},{label:`Left at Centennial after the round trip on full tanks (gallons)`,answer:23.6,tol:0.1}],
     solution:[`Down: 80 ÷ 118 = 0.678 hours × 60 = 40.7 minutes. Fuel 0.678 × 8.0 = 5.42, plus 2.0 = 7.4 gallons.`,`Reserve: 45 minutes is 0.75 × 8.0 = 6.0 gallons. Legal minimum to leave: 7.4 + 6.0 = 13.4 gallons.`,`Home: 80 ÷ 92 = 0.870 hours × 60 = 52.2 minutes. Fuel 0.870 × 8.0 = 6.96, plus 2.0 = 9.0 gallons. Legal minimum to leave Pueblo: 9.0 + 6.0 = 15.0.`,`Walt's case: 51.2 ÷ 92 = 0.557 hours, and 51.2 ÷ 118 = 0.434 hours. Together 0.990 hours × 8.0 = 7.92, plus 2.0 for taxi and climb = 9.9, plus 6.0 reserve = 15.9 gallons. Leaving Pueblo with 32.6 covers it twice.`,`Full tanks: 40 minus 7.4 minus 9.0 = 23.6 gallons at home, about 3 hours. That's a column Walt signs.`]}
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f10s8`, title:`First moves`, intro:`Marisol wrote these on the hangar whiteboard under Danny's capitals and her own, and made Jordan answer them out loud while she put the cowling back on. Pick the first move for each.`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`The engine quits at 3,000 feet AGL.`, choices:[`Pitch for best glide, 65 knots`,`Try a restart right away`,`Call ATC first`], answer:0, why:`Fly the airplane first. Best glide buys the most distance and time for everything else.`},
      {prompt:`Roughness and an RPM drop on a cool, damp night in a carbureted engine.`, choices:[`Mixture full lean`,`Carburetor heat on`,`Master switch off`], answer:1, why:`Suspect carburetor ice. Apply full carb heat and leave it on. The roughness may get worse briefly as the ice melts.`},
      {prompt:`Smoke and orange light from under the cowling in flight.`, choices:[`Open the vents`,`Master switch off`,`Mixture idle cutoff, fuel off`], answer:2, why:`AFH: shut off fuel to the engine first, with the mixture at idle cutoff and the fuel selector off, ignition on.`},
      {prompt:`A smell of hot insulation, no visible fire.`, choices:[`Isolate the circuit or master off`,`Open the windows wide`,`Climb to a safer altitude`], answer:0, why:`An electrical fire: find and isolate the faulty circuit, or turn off the master if flight conditions permit, and land as soon as possible.`},
      {prompt:`Smoke in the cabin after you've used the extinguisher.`, choices:[`Close every vent`,`Open the vents to clear it`,`Climb to thin the smoke`], answer:1, why:`After the extinguisher, open the cabin vents to clear smoke. Close them again if the smoke gets worse.`},
      {prompt:`The ammeter shows a steady discharge at night.`, choices:[`Master off to save power`,`Shed load, land at nearest suitable`,`Continue and watch it`], answer:1, why:`The alternator has failed. Shed nonessential loads, notify ATC, and land at the nearest suitable airport.`},
      {prompt:`The left door pops open with a bang on rotation.`, choices:[`Fly the airplane`,`Reach over and close it`,`Abort the climb at once`], answer:0, why:`AFH: an open door seldom affects the airplane's ability to fly. Concentrate on flying, then land and close it.`},
      {prompt:`At night the ground lights fade and you're in snow.`, choices:[`Descend to find the lights`,`Wings level on the attitude indicator`,`Look outside for the horizon`], answer:1, why:`AFH inadvertent IMC: the only concern at first is keeping the wings level by the instruments. Then a gentle turn back, and ask ATC for help.`},
      {prompt:`You're talking to Approach with an assigned code when trouble starts.`, choices:[`Squawk 7700 immediately`,`Keep the code and tell them`,`Squawk 7600`], answer:1, why:`AIM 6-3-2: keep the assigned code while in contact with ATC unless told otherwise. 7700 is for when you can't establish contact.`},
      {prompt:`You've become unsure of your fuel and position.`, choices:[`Wait until it's certain`,`Ask for help now`,`Climb and keep quiet`], answer:1, why:`AIM 6-1-2: you're in at least an urgency condition the moment you become doubtful. That's the time to ask.`},
      {prompt:`After a hard landing, before shutting down.`, choices:[`Listen on the emergency frequency`,`Test the ELT for three sweeps`,`Turn the ELT off for the night`], answer:0, why:`AIM 6-2-4 recommends monitoring the emergency frequency before engine shutdown at the end of each flight to catch an inadvertent ELT activation.`},
      {prompt:`An engine failure right after takeoff, low.`, choices:[`Turn back to the runway`,`Land mostly straight ahead`,`Climb to gain altitude`], answer:1, why:`AFH: continuing straight ahead or with a slight turn is usually the lowest risk option. Turning back requires altitude and practice you may not have.`}
    ]},
  quiz: [
    {q:`By 14 CFR 1.1, night is the time between`, opts:[`sunset and sunrise, converted to local time`,`one hour after sunset and one hour before sunrise`,`end of evening and start of morning civil twilight`], a:2, why:`1.1 defines night by civil twilight, as published in the Air Almanac and converted to local time. Sunset to sunrise is the 91.209 lighting period. One hour after sunset is the 61.57(b) passenger currency period.`, task:`XI`, domain:`Navigation and emergencies`},
    {q:`To carry passengers under 61.57(b) from 1 hour after sunset to 1 hour before sunrise, a pilot needs, within the preceding 90 days and during that same period, at least`, opts:[`3 takeoffs and 3 landings to a full stop`,`3 takeoffs and 3 landings of any kind`,`5 takeoffs and 5 landings to a full stop`], a:0, why:`61.57(b)(1): three takeoffs and three landings to a full stop during the period 1 hour after sunset to 1 hour before sunrise, as sole manipulator, in the same category, class, and type if required.`, task:`I.A`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`For a private pilot certificate with an airplane single-engine rating, the night training must include`, opts:[`5 hours, including a 150 NM solo cross-country`,`3 hours, a cross-country over 100 NM, and 10 full stops`,`3 hours, including 3 full stops at a towered airport`], a:1, why:`61.109(a)(2): 3 hours of night flight training with one cross-country over 100 NM total distance and 10 takeoffs and 10 full-stop landings, each with a flight in the traffic pattern.`, task:`I.A`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`A VFR flight at night will take 1 hour 30 minutes to the first point of intended landing at 8 gallons per hour. The minimum fuel required by 91.151 is`, opts:[`16 gallons`,`18 gallons`,`20 gallons`], a:1, why:`Flight: 1.5 × 8 = 12 gallons. Night reserve: 45 minutes × 8 gal/hr = 0.75 × 8 = 6 gallons. 12 + 6 = 18. 16 would be the day reserve.`, task:`I.D`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`Under 91.209, an aircraft operated between sunset and sunrise must have`, opts:[`its landing light lit`,`lighted position lights`,`its taxi light off`], a:1, why:`91.209(a)(1) requires lighted position lights from sunset to sunrise. Anticollision lights must be on if installed, though the PIC may turn them off in the interest of safety.`, task:`I.B`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`On final, a four-light PAPI shows three white lights and one red light. The airplane is`, opts:[`slightly above the glide path`,`on the glide path`,`slightly below the glide path`], a:0, why:`Two white and two red is on the glide path. More white is high, more red is low. Three white and one red is slightly high.`, task:`III.A`, domain:`Airport and flight`},
    {q:`To turn on pilot controlled runway lighting at the highest intensity, the pilot should key the microphone`, opts:[`3 times within 5 seconds`,`5 times within 3 seconds`,`7 times within 5 seconds`], a:2, why:`AIM 2-1-8: 7 clicks within 5 seconds for the highest intensity, 5 for medium, 3 for low. The AIM recommends always keying 7 first. The lights stay on 15 minutes.`, task:`III.A`, domain:`Airport and flight`},
    {q:`At night, the best way to see a dim object is to`, opts:[`stare directly at it`,`look slightly to one side of it`,`use a bright white flashlight`], a:1, why:`The fovea has no rods, which creates a night blind spot. Off-center viewing about 10 degrees from the object uses the rods. Bright light destroys dark adaptation.`, task:`XI`, domain:`Navigation and emergencies`},
    {q:`At night you see another airplane's red position light on your right and its green light on your left. The other airplane is`, opts:[`flying away from you`,`flying the same direction`,`approaching head-on`], a:2, why:`Red is on the left wingtip and green on the right. Seeing red on your right and green on your left means you're looking at its nose: a possible collision course (AFH chapter 11).`, task:`XI`, domain:`Navigation and emergencies`},
    {q:`An approach at night over dark, featureless terrain toward a lighted runway tends to make a pilot fly`, opts:[`a lower than normal approach`,`a higher than normal approach`,`a normal approach with a long float`], a:0, why:`AIM 8-1-5: featureless terrain creates the illusion of being higher than you are, so the pilot who doesn't recognize it flies low. That's the black hole approach.`, task:`I.H`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`Unless the POH says otherwise, the first action for an engine compartment fire in flight is to`, opts:[`turn off the master switch`,`shut off fuel to the engine`,`open the cabin air vents`], a:1, why:`AFH chapter 18: mixture to idle cutoff and fuel selector off, ignition left on. Turning off the master first costs radios and the transponder unless the fire is electrical.`, task:`IX`, domain:`Navigation and emergencies`},
    {q:`A pilot already in radio contact with ATC and squawking an assigned code has an emergency. The AIM says to`, opts:[`keep the code ATC assigned`,`switch to 7700 immediately`,`switch to 7600 and stay quiet`], a:0, why:`AIM 6-3-2: continue squawking the assigned code when in contact with ATC unless instructed otherwise. Squawk 7700 if you can't immediately establish communications.`, task:`IX`, domain:`Navigation and emergencies`},
    {q:`A pilot without an instrument rating, flying at night, sees the ground lights fade and realizes the airplane is in cloud. The first priority is to`, opts:[`level the wings on the attitude indicator`,`descend until the ground lights come back`,`turn back right away with a steep bank`], a:0, why:`AFH, inadvertent VFR into IMC: the only concern at first is keeping the wings level. Fly the attitude indicator with your fingertips. Once it's under control, make a gentle, shallow 180 degree turn, about standard rate, back toward the clear air you came from, tell ATC you aren't instrument rated, and ask for help. Descending blind at night is how pilots hit terrain, and a steep bank on instruments invites a spiral.`, task:`VIII`, domain:`Airport and flight`},
    {q:`Spaced review, performance. Field elevation 4,729 feet, altimeter 30.22. The pressure altitude is about`, opts:[`5,029 feet`,`4,729 feet`,`4,429 feet`], a:2, why:`Pressure altitude = field elevation + (29.92 minus altimeter) × 1,000 = 4,729 + (29.92 minus 30.22) × 1,000 = 4,729 minus 300 = 4,429 feet.`, task:`I.F`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`Spaced review, airspace. In Class G airspace at night, 1,200 feet AGL or less, outside an airport traffic pattern, the VFR minimums are`, opts:[`1 SM and clear of clouds`,`3 SM, 500 below, 1,000 above, 2,000 horizontal`,`5 SM, 1,000 below, 1,000 above, 1 SM horizontal`], a:1, why:`91.155: Class G at night, 1,200 ft AGL or less, is 3 SM and 500 below, 1,000 above, 2,000 horizontal from clouds (an airplane may operate clear of clouds with 1 SM inside a half mile of the runway in the pattern). 1 SM and clear of clouds is the day rule.`, task:`I.E`, domain:`Preflight`}
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>{
    const p=(s.picks||{})[11];
    const went=!!(s.flags.pushedweather||G.pushedweather);
    const waited=(p===2);
    const leave=went?`6:20`:(waited?`6:05`:`5:20`);
    return [
    (went
      ?`Engine off, the cylinders ticking. Somebody else was taxiing out, God knew why, and snow went sideways through their landing light. Abby finally talked. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"I told you I'd fly what you decided. Dumb promise. On this night especially. That part's on me."</span> She glanced at the card over the mag switch and away. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Mine's macho. Walt wrote it on the board and I didn't look at it."</span> And then, not looking at Jordan at all: <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"We got lucky. Put that in the logbook in those words, or in a month you'll remember it as flying."</span>`
      :(waited
        ?`6:05, the defroster on full and losing, the cake box belted into the passenger seat like a kid. She was an hour behind where she could have been. Abby hadn't mentioned the hour once. Abby counted everything, so that was its own comment.`
        :`Abby walked her to the hangar. Neither of them said anything about the flight that wasn't going to happen, which Jordan understood was a kind of respect. The tanks were full. They'd stay full. Russ had Biscuit Monday, and Russ would be delighted.`)),
    `Walt was in the hangar. He hadn't said he'd be there. He was sitting on Marisol's rolling stool, which Jordan had never seen anybody but Marisol sit on, in his canvas jacket with two coffees from the FBO on the workbench beside him and his reading glasses folded in his shirt pocket. He'd been watching the weather on his phone, the way he must have watched it for thirty years, and he'd seen the PIREP. He'd known it was her night. He held out one of the coffees.`,
    (went
      ?`<span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Heard you on Approach."</span> He tipped his head toward the lot. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Scanner in the truck. Thirty years, you don't quit."</span> He held the coffee out until she took it, two hands. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Sit. There's something I've been not telling you since April."</span>`
      :`<span class="said" data-who="Walt">"You're not going,"</span> he said. It wasn't a question, and he didn't sound pleased or surprised. He sounded like a man who'd been waiting at a gate to see which way somebody would walk. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Sit down a minute before you drive. There's something I've been not telling you since April."</span>`),
    `She sat on an upturned bucket. Walt looked at the coffee instead of at her.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Walt" data-tone="Quiet, slow, very even.">"Winter of ninety six. I was thirty four. Two years on the desk at a regional outfit that doesn't exist anymore. A thirty-some seat turboprop, eleven people in the back. A night run into a mountain town I'm not going to name. The forecast at the destination was above minimums, with snow showers in the vicinity. Legal. The alternate was legal. The fuel was legal, and then some. On an airline, the captain and the dispatcher both sign the release. Both of us own it."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Walt" data-tone="Quiet, slow, very even.">"I had a feeling about those showers. I didn't have a reason. I had a feeling, and a forecast that said yes, and a station that kept calling because there were people waiting for a wedding. So I signed. The captain signed."</span> He turned the cup a quarter turn on the bench. <span class="said" data-who="Walt" data-tone="Quiet, slow, very even.">"They didn't come home."</span>`,
    `The hangar heater ticked. Somewhere outside, a truck went by on the service road with its chains on.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Walt" data-tone="Quiet, slow, very even.">"The report said what those reports say. It talked about the descent and the snow and the terrain. It didn't have my name in it. It didn't need to. The release did."</span> He finally looked at her. <span class="said" data-who="Walt" data-tone="Quiet, slow, very even.">"I've spent thirty years since then telling people things they didn't want to hear about the weather. I told myself it was the job. It was the eleven people. The forecast is permission. That's all it ever is. I treated it like a reason."</span>`,
    (went
      ?`<span class="said" data-who="Walt">"You went. It went bad. And you're sitting on my bucket anyway."</span> A sip. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"That's the other ending. I'd have taken it, back then, if anybody was handing it out."</span> He got up the way he got up from everything, in stages. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Don't make a habit of needing it. We'll talk about the rest when you've earned the drive home."</span>`
      :`<span class="said" data-who="Walt" data-tone="Quiet, steady, deeply felt.">"I wanted the share sold because I didn't think you'd be able to do what you just did. Most people can't. Most pilots can't. Your brother couldn't, and I loved your brother."</span> He stood, slowly, the way he did everything. <span class="said" data-who="Walt" data-tone="Quiet, steady, deeply felt.">"You turned it down with the tanks full and your family waiting. I've spent thirty years waiting to see somebody do that without me telling them to."</span>`),
    `He picked up his coffee and went out the side door to his truck, and didn't look back, and Jordan understood that was the only way he could have said any of it.`,
    `Car running, heater on high and doing nothing yet, snow collecting in the corners of the windshield where the wipers didn't reach. She dug the green notebook out of her flight bag. Folded corner. His, after hers. She'd made her call, so now he got his.`,
    `<prop type="doc" title="Danny's logbook, a page from December, five years ago">Dec 11. KPUB to KAPA, night. 1.1 on the Hobbs. Ma's 58th at Velarde's. Left at 9:40 because Tia wouldn't stop bringing flan.<br>TAF said the snow came after midnight. It came at ten. By Pueblo it was nothing, by the Springs it was low, by Monument it was the road. Followed the I-25 headlights over the hill at what I am going to write down as 800 feet and was not 800 feet. Lost the ground. I don't know how long. Long enough to know what it feels like. Came out over Larkspur with the wings level only because I was too scared to move them.<br>Landed. Sat in the car an hour with the engine running. Called Ma from the car and told her it was a beautiful night.<br>Got lucky. Never again. Don't tell Mom.<br>(Next year leave Velarde's by 7. Beat it home.)</prop>`,
    `Twice through. Then the parentheses again, because of course. Never again, and one line down, how to keep doing it anyway. Leave by 7, beat it home. Every year after that he left a little earlier, and her mother thought it was because he worked in the morning.`,
    (went
      ?`Her hands had a shake in them. Same as after a bad call, an hour on, when the adrenaline finally has somewhere to go. Larkspur. She'd been over Larkspur tonight, watching the highway go soft. He'd been alone up there. She'd had Abby saying my airplane in that flat voice. He'd had to write got lucky himself. Jordan's, Abby had said out loud for her on the ramp.`
      :`She didn't start the wipers. An hour ago she'd been sitting in his airplane looking south at his ridge, same lid on it, same snow coming. And she'd turned around at the tie down. Which, it turned out, was the cheap place to do it.`),
    `She sat there furious with him, the snow filling in the glass, and for once she let herself have it. He'd called their mother from a parking lot and told her it was a beautiful night. He got lucky on that hill and kept flying it every December, just earlier, like the clock was the problem, and then he died on a Saturday morning of the one thing nobody could have turned around for.`,
    `Her mother wasn't getting the page. Don't tell Mom. Okay, Danny. Fine. She could do that one.`,
    `She called to say she was leaving instead. ${leave} on the dash clock. First ring.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Lourdes">"Mija."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"I'm driving, Ma. I'm leaving now. I'll be late."</span>`,
    `A long silence. Then: <span class="said" data-who="Lourdes">"Okay. Go slow on the hill. They're saying it's bad on the hill."</span>`,
    `Her mother was right about the hill. Past Castle Rock the snow came straight into the headlights, every semi had its flashers going, and somewhere ahead a plow kept throwing orange light across the median. Thirty five, both hands, radio off, a Subaru riding her bumper for six miles until it gave up. Once, near the top, she looked up at the ridge and the gray sitting on it and thought about a Skylane at 8,500 under that. Then road again.`,
    `${went?`Ten to nine when she got to Velarde's.`:(waited?`Half past eight when she got to Velarde's.`:`Twenty after seven when she got to Velarde's.`)} Somebody had propped the back room door open with a chair and it was still about ninety degrees in there. Green chile, an aunt's perfume, a space heater nobody could find the switch for. Balloons on the last chair, one already sagging. Ellie's sign was by the door, one corner already curling off the tape. Airplane, backward 4. Below it, added this afternoon in a purple from some other marker, a car with wings.`,
    `Her mother had the head of the table and the good maroon cardigan, sixty three as of this morning, and hadn't touched her enchiladas. The phone lay face up by the bread basket. Eleven years of waiters had known to leave it there.`,
    `For a while her mother just looked at her. A cousin at the far end quit talking mid-story. Jordan was still in the doorway, dripping, holding the cake box out with both hands like a delivery she needed signed for.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Lourdes" data-tone="Quiet, stunned, wondering.">"You drove."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Jordan" data-tone="Soft, steady, a little choked.">"I drove, Ma."</span>`,
    `Her mother flipped the phone. Screen down on the tablecloth, next to the bread. Half the table missed it, and the half that saw it mostly thought she was being polite. <span class="said" data-who="man">"Who called?"</span> somebody's uncle wanted to know. Claire had a hand pressed flat over her mouth.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Lourdes" data-tone="Steady, carrying, quietly proud.">"Eleven years I've sat at this table waiting for one of my kids to come down out of the sky,"</span> her mother said, to nobody in particular, to all of them. <span class="said" data-who="Lourdes" data-tone="Steady, carrying, quietly proud.">"This is the first time one of my kids ever turned around."</span>`,
    `${went?`Jordan let it go. Technically she had turned around. At the very end, in the snow, with Abby's hands on the yoke. She'd take it, and later she'd make it count.`:`Jordan didn't say anything. Her coat was dripping on the floor.`} She got the cake down a second before Ellie hit her in the knees, already mid-question. Did the car part work. Was it upslope, because Mom said upslope on the phone like twice. Was Biscuit scared. Yes, yes, and no, she's tied down, and on a night like this that's about the best a plane can hope for. Ellie took that in and asked when the flan was coming.`,
    `Flan, finally. Ellie got the burnt part, which had been negotiated in the car. Candles, then the song, English first, and on the Spanish verse the waiters came in so loud a busboy dropped a fork. Somewhere in there her mother reached over and took Jordan's hand on the tablecloth. She kept it through the coffee, eating left handed to do it. Nobody mentioned it.`,
    `At 10:52 PM, in her aunt's spare room, with an alarm set for 4:30 so she could follow the plows home and make the station by seven, her phone buzzed.`,
    `<prop type="text" from="Abby Strand" time="10:52 PM">${(s.score>=27)?`Rebooked. Wednesday the 16th, Pueblo, night cross-country, clear and cold all day. Your mother can have a second birthday. Then the long solo, then the written. Book the test for January. You're ready for it. You were ready for tonight too, which is the part that matters.`:`Rebooked. Wednesday the 16th, Pueblo, night cross-country, clear and cold all day. We're going to talk about a few things from the last three weeks first. Then the long solo, then the written. Book the test for January. Tonight was a hard one. Get some sleep.`}</prop>`
    ];
  },
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch10.mp3`, len:`about 60 minutes`},
  next:`The Written`
});
